WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.187 --> 00:00:09.882
(speaks in foreign language)

00:00:09.882 --> 00:00:13.210
- Hello everybody, and
welcome to this webinar

00:00:13.210 --> 00:00:15.730
on statues and statutes,

00:00:15.730 --> 00:00:17.810
a round-table on the French Caribbean

00:00:17.810 --> 00:00:19.860
in the age of hashtag #MustFall

00:00:20.930 --> 00:00:23.213
and hashtag #BlackLivesMatter.

00:00:24.490 --> 00:00:26.310
Let me begin by acknowledging

00:00:26.310 --> 00:00:28.730
that indigenous peoples and nations,

00:00:28.730 --> 00:00:33.440
including Mohegan, Mashantucket
Pequot, Eastern Pequot,

00:00:33.440 --> 00:00:37.650
Schaghticoke, Golden
Hill Paugusset, Niantic

00:00:37.650 --> 00:00:41.050
and the Quinnipiac and other
Algonquian-speaking peoples,

00:00:41.050 --> 00:00:44.970
had to ward it through
generations the lands

00:00:44.970 --> 00:00:49.480
and waterways of what is now
the State of Connecticut.

00:00:49.480 --> 00:00:53.110
We honor and respect the
enduring relationship that exists

00:00:53.110 --> 00:00:56.910
between these peoples and
nations and this land.

00:00:56.910 --> 00:00:59.040
I also acknowledge that we meet today

00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:01.840
within an institution that has benefited

00:01:01.840 --> 00:01:05.613
from the enslaved labor
of African men and women.

00:01:07.470 --> 00:01:09.360
My name is Cécile Fromont,

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:12.540
and I am on the Faculty of
The History of Art Department

00:01:12.540 --> 00:01:14.680
at Yale where I teach African

00:01:14.680 --> 00:01:16.623
and Latin American art history.

00:01:17.770 --> 00:01:20.290
I'm here today in this capacity,

00:01:20.290 --> 00:01:24.020
but also as a member of the
French Caribbean diaspora.

00:01:24.020 --> 00:01:26.930
As many of my compatriots,
I was born and raised

00:01:26.930 --> 00:01:29.150
in Martinique, but left the island

00:01:29.150 --> 00:01:32.610
to pursue higher education in Europe,

00:01:32.610 --> 00:01:34.503
and really never returned.

00:01:36.900 --> 00:01:39.827
First, let me say a word
about the organization

00:01:39.827 --> 00:01:43.510
of our time together this
afternoon or morning.

00:01:43.510 --> 00:01:47.640
I will begin by introducing
our panelists and the session,

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:50.480
and they will then share some remarks

00:01:50.480 --> 00:01:52.233
for about 10 minutes each.

00:01:53.240 --> 00:01:56.150
This will be followed by
a round-table discussion

00:01:57.090 --> 00:02:00.030
the three of us for 30 minutes.

00:02:00.030 --> 00:02:01.880
We will then open the floor

00:02:01.880 --> 00:02:05.750
to Q&amp;A for the remainder
of our time together.

00:02:05.750 --> 00:02:10.750
We invite you to ask questions
using the Q&amp;A function

00:02:11.040 --> 00:02:13.570
in Zoom at any time.

00:02:13.570 --> 00:02:17.430
I will read the questions to
the panelists and the audience

00:02:17.430 --> 00:02:22.220
once we get to the Q&amp;A
part of our time together.

00:02:22.220 --> 00:02:26.220
If you include your name and
any relevant backgrounds,

00:02:26.220 --> 00:02:28.773
I will share that too.

00:02:31.230 --> 00:02:34.860
Before going any further, let
me thank Prof. Stephen Pitti

00:02:34.860 --> 00:02:37.480
and the Rhythm (muffled speaking) Yale

00:02:37.480 --> 00:02:40.890
for offering a forum to this conversation.

00:02:40.890 --> 00:02:44.480
And thanks in particular
to its Associate Director,

00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:48.945
Matthew Tanico, for making
the event technically

00:02:48.945 --> 00:02:51.083
and intellectually possible.

00:02:52.560 --> 00:02:55.970
I thank also everyone of
you for joining us today

00:02:55.970 --> 00:03:00.053
for what we hope will be a
constructive conversation.

00:03:01.090 --> 00:03:05.077
I'm most grateful of course
to Dr. Audrey Celestine

00:03:05.077 --> 00:03:08.920
and Zaka Toto for responding
positively to my call

00:03:08.920 --> 00:03:11.263
to participate in this forum.

00:03:13.330 --> 00:03:16.990
So many of you gathered here
today (muffled speaking)

00:03:16.990 --> 00:03:19.770
much more than I do about the topic,

00:03:19.770 --> 00:03:23.500
but for those among us
who are maybe not familiar

00:03:23.500 --> 00:03:28.500
with Martinique and its
history, I have prepared

00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:33.110
the shortest of introductions
about the island,

00:03:33.110 --> 00:03:38.110
focusing on the protagonist
that you will here about

00:03:40.020 --> 00:03:42.163
in the rest of the discussion.

00:03:44.330 --> 00:03:48.610
So Martinique is a small
island in the Caribbean,

00:03:48.610 --> 00:03:53.213
it's about in the middle of
the art, that has been French,

00:03:54.130 --> 00:03:59.130
so to speak, since 1635 with
the establishment on the island

00:04:00.100 --> 00:04:02.613
of a settlement by
Pierre Belain d'Esnambuc,

00:04:03.930 --> 00:04:06.750
this pirate-looking guy here.

00:04:06.750 --> 00:04:11.410
In 1636, as part of his plan,

00:04:11.410 --> 00:04:16.410
the first enslaved people
joined the island, were brought

00:04:16.620 --> 00:04:20.420
to the island, and tensions soon rose

00:04:20.420 --> 00:04:25.290
with the indigenous populations
leading to the massacre

00:04:25.290 --> 00:04:29.333
of Caribs and Arawak Amerindians in 1658.

00:04:31.110 --> 00:04:35.120
The island would remain a
sugarcane plantation colony

00:04:35.120 --> 00:04:40.120
for the following
centuries, relying heavily

00:04:40.660 --> 00:04:42.890
on enslaved labor.

00:04:42.890 --> 00:04:46.530
The first abolition of
slavery in the French Empire

00:04:46.530 --> 00:04:51.380
in 1794 did not reach the
island whose planter class

00:04:51.380 --> 00:04:53.320
had sided with the British

00:04:53.320 --> 00:04:58.320
at the time in part to
avoid that abolition.

00:05:00.410 --> 00:05:05.300
In 1804, Josephine de
Beauharnais, the wife of Napoleon

00:05:05.300 --> 00:05:09.080
becomes Empress of France,
and a powerful spokesperson

00:05:09.080 --> 00:05:11.373
on behalf of the planters' course.

00:05:12.230 --> 00:05:15.030
She's associated in
Martinique and elsewhere

00:05:15.030 --> 00:05:18.660
in the Caribbean with their
institution of slavery

00:05:18.660 --> 00:05:20.053
in the French Empire.

00:05:23.680 --> 00:05:28.680
Abolition would be ceased, not
bestowed, on May 22nd, 1848

00:05:29.580 --> 00:05:32.700
in Martinique by the enslaved themselves

00:05:32.700 --> 00:05:36.440
who was up against slavery in anticipation

00:05:36.440 --> 00:05:40.220
of the too long awaited decree
from the French Assembly

00:05:40.220 --> 00:05:43.713
that had declared
emancipation weeks before,

00:05:44.580 --> 00:05:47.770
a decree closely associated
with French politician

00:05:47.770 --> 00:05:50.093
and abolitionist Victor Schoelcher.

00:05:52.850 --> 00:05:56.470
In 1946, in the aftermath of World War II,

00:05:56.470 --> 00:05:58.670
and under the leadership of Aime Cesaire,

00:05:58.670 --> 00:06:01.290
Deputy and Mayor of Fort-de-France,

00:06:01.290 --> 00:06:06.150
the capital of Martinique
from 1945 to 2001,

00:06:06.150 --> 00:06:08.450
Martinique became alongside a small number

00:06:08.450 --> 00:06:13.200
of French so-called old
colonies, a department of French.

00:06:13.200 --> 00:06:18.200
It joined the sovereignty of
the French State in full form.

00:06:20.260 --> 00:06:23.850
That meant that it was no longer a colony,

00:06:23.850 --> 00:06:27.473
and thus it gains its current statutes.

00:06:28.690 --> 00:06:31.830
As a teenager, I remember asking Cesaire,

00:06:31.830 --> 00:06:35.923
why departmentalization
and not independence?

00:06:36.950 --> 00:06:40.390
With the look that I now with
the benefit of age recognize

00:06:40.390 --> 00:06:44.290
as annoyed benevolence
his eyes stared at me

00:06:44.290 --> 00:06:46.810
and answered, ask your grandparents.

00:06:46.810 --> 00:06:48.010
That's what they wanted.

00:06:50.240 --> 00:06:54.200
Notably, as part of a large
project of urban renewal

00:06:54.200 --> 00:06:58.680
for Fort-de-France, Cesaire
chose not to remove the statues

00:06:58.680 --> 00:07:02.610
of Josephine, Schoelcher or
d'Esnambuc, but to move them

00:07:02.610 --> 00:07:06.020
to less prominent locations
where they received rounds

00:07:06.020 --> 00:07:09.970
after rounds of vernacular interventions,

00:07:09.970 --> 00:07:12.993
defacing, spray-painting, et cetera.

00:07:14.470 --> 00:07:18.210
Josephine famously lost the head in 1991,

00:07:18.210 --> 00:07:20.720
and has since been the site of street art

00:07:20.720 --> 00:07:23.050
and militant intervention.

00:07:23.050 --> 00:07:26.180
She's often seen, or she
was often seen doused

00:07:26.180 --> 00:07:28.550
in red bloods on the right

00:07:28.550 --> 00:07:31.113
with the (speaking in
foreign language) et cetera.

00:07:33.930 --> 00:07:37.827
In recent decades, the
political (muffled speaking)

00:07:37.827 --> 00:07:40.130
has been marked by
increased technicization

00:07:40.130 --> 00:07:43.620
of political debates
and a relative inability

00:07:43.620 --> 00:07:48.380
of local political forces to
propose a political project

00:07:48.380 --> 00:07:53.080
in a context of demographic
decline, high unemployment,

00:07:53.080 --> 00:07:56.460
increasing inequalities
and the health scandals

00:07:56.460 --> 00:08:00.440
surrounding the poisoning of
the islands with chlordecone,

00:08:00.440 --> 00:08:03.610
an insecticide used in banana agriculture

00:08:03.610 --> 00:08:06.900
which was forbidden
because of its toxicity

00:08:06.900 --> 00:08:09.960
in all the France and the European Union,

00:08:09.960 --> 00:08:12.520
except in the French Caribbean

00:08:12.520 --> 00:08:14.610
by special delegation granted

00:08:14.610 --> 00:08:15.983
to the planters' lobby.

00:08:18.920 --> 00:08:22.530
In the summer of 2020, as
the City of Fort-de-France

00:08:22.530 --> 00:08:24.470
had already decided to remove

00:08:24.470 --> 00:08:27.540
increasingly-contested monuments,

00:08:27.540 --> 00:08:30.120
militants associated by all accounts

00:08:30.120 --> 00:08:32.340
with the (speaking in
foreign language) Movement

00:08:32.340 --> 00:08:35.653
named after the
independence movement flag.

00:08:38.236 --> 00:08:41.843
Schoelcher, Josephine and d'Esnambuc.

00:08:45.030 --> 00:08:46.850
The dramatic gestures spurred

00:08:46.850 --> 00:08:49.460
an impassion debate in Martinique.

00:08:49.460 --> 00:08:52.380
And today, it is the
stakes and significance

00:08:52.380 --> 00:08:55.120
of this gestures and
of their interpretation

00:08:55.120 --> 00:08:57.773
that will be the topic of our round-table.

00:09:00.410 --> 00:09:03.420
Without further ado,
let me introduce to you

00:09:03.420 --> 00:09:07.223
the two panelists before
we hear from them.

00:09:09.750 --> 00:09:11.650
First, I'm delighted to introduce

00:09:11.650 --> 00:09:15.300
to you Audrey Celestine,
who is a social scientist

00:09:15.300 --> 00:09:17.970
and an associate (muffled
speaking) studies

00:09:17.970 --> 00:09:21.640
at the University of Lille
in the North of France.

00:09:21.640 --> 00:09:26.640
Her work focuses on migration,
memory, race and identity

00:09:27.070 --> 00:09:31.830
in France, in the French
Caribbean and the United States.

00:09:31.830 --> 00:09:34.610
Her first book, "La Fabrique
Politique des Identites",

00:09:34.610 --> 00:09:36.720
deals with the collective
(muffled speaking)

00:09:36.720 --> 00:09:40.300
of Puerto Ricans in New York
and French Caribbeans in Paris

00:09:40.300 --> 00:09:42.283
since the mid 20th century.

00:09:43.750 --> 00:09:47.350
It was published in 2018 by Karthala.

00:09:47.350 --> 00:09:49.470
She also published "Une Famille Francaise

00:09:49.470 --> 00:09:52.070
Des Antilles a Dunkerque
en Passant par l'Algerie"

00:09:52.070 --> 00:09:57.070
in 2018 on the impact of
migration, exile, colonization,

00:09:57.250 --> 00:10:00.960
race and identity in the
individual trajectories

00:10:00.960 --> 00:10:04.253
of four generations of an
ordinary French family.

00:10:05.250 --> 00:10:07.567
She also recently published

00:10:07.567 --> 00:10:10.410
"Des Vies de Combat Femmes,
Noires et Libres" in 2020.

00:10:15.390 --> 00:10:20.390
Our second panelist is Zaka
Toto, who is in his own words,

00:10:20.910 --> 00:10:24.170
a writer and entrepreneur alternatively

00:10:24.170 --> 00:10:26.200
or at the same time.

00:10:26.200 --> 00:10:28.053
Depends, he says.

00:10:28.900 --> 00:10:32.760
Based in Martinique, he
created the online review ZIST

00:10:32.760 --> 00:10:37.370
in 2018, with writers, scholars,
photographers, creators

00:10:37.370 --> 00:10:40.630
from across the black diaspora,
with most of the funding

00:10:40.630 --> 00:10:42.293
coming from his dad's garden.

00:10:43.210 --> 00:10:46.330
He writes about Martinique
in the Caribbean mostly,

00:10:46.330 --> 00:10:48.303
and the current global zeitgeists.

00:10:49.300 --> 00:10:52.480
He's obsessed with cities,
how you walk and live in them,

00:10:52.480 --> 00:10:56.300
how the experience of a specific
place relates to culture,

00:10:56.300 --> 00:10:58.543
policy and social economics.

00:10:59.800 --> 00:11:03.390
This obsession came to form
in 2017 when he started

00:11:03.390 --> 00:11:04.900
a comparative writing project

00:11:04.900 --> 00:11:08.630
around colonial ideologies and
legacies, how they translate

00:11:08.630 --> 00:11:11.960
into urban spaces and the
cultural dynamics involved

00:11:11.960 --> 00:11:14.010
in different cases,

00:11:14.010 --> 00:11:15.970
linking the protest in Charlottesville

00:11:15.970 --> 00:11:18.600
to what happened in
Martinique in an earlier era,

00:11:18.600 --> 00:11:22.363
but also Algeria,
Paraguayan, China and Taiwan.

00:11:23.380 --> 00:11:26.540
Finally, this year, he
concluded in the series

00:11:28.860 --> 00:11:31.773
that the most colonial
place of all is Paris.

00:11:32.900 --> 00:11:36.210
He also made the mistake,
he says, of studying

00:11:36.210 --> 00:11:39.810
a PhD in History at the
University (indistinct)

00:11:39.810 --> 00:11:43.153
on political cultures and
identity in Martinique.

00:11:44.810 --> 00:11:48.820
He's also foolish
enough, he says, to think

00:11:48.820 --> 00:11:51.010
that he can start urban
renovation programs

00:11:51.010 --> 00:11:55.453
in Fort-de-France and build
one of (indistinct) old dream.

00:11:56.600 --> 00:12:01.600
So let's now hear from our two panelists.

00:12:02.370 --> 00:12:04.410
- Well, thank you very much Cecile

00:12:04.410 --> 00:12:08.720
for the invitation and the
opportunity to have a discussion

00:12:08.720 --> 00:12:11.140
with you and with Zaka.

00:12:11.140 --> 00:12:12.180
We've been talking a lot

00:12:12.180 --> 00:12:15.160
about this topics in the last year,

00:12:15.160 --> 00:12:18.950
and even more so during the
last six months that I'm happy

00:12:18.950 --> 00:12:23.080
to have this new arena to talk
about all of these issues.

00:12:23.080 --> 00:12:26.060
And I think you were,
the summary or synthesis

00:12:26.060 --> 00:12:28.870
that you provided at the
beginning of your talk

00:12:28.870 --> 00:12:33.450
as an introduction was, that
it is actually very important

00:12:33.450 --> 00:12:36.920
because we can definitely see the events

00:12:36.920 --> 00:12:39.267
of this summer, not the
summer in Martinique,

00:12:39.267 --> 00:12:42.940
but of the summer 2020, as a sort

00:12:42.940 --> 00:12:47.940
of Martinique conversion of
a more global phenomenon.

00:12:48.120 --> 00:12:50.950
But I actually think it's
really important to place it

00:12:50.950 --> 00:12:55.570
into the very local localized
perspective and to put it

00:12:55.570 --> 00:13:00.570
within the framework of politics
and society in Martinique

00:13:01.490 --> 00:13:03.830
since the departmentalization of 1946.

00:13:06.120 --> 00:13:08.940
As you said, in 1946,
representatives from Martinique,

00:13:08.940 --> 00:13:12.250
Guadeloupe but also French
Guiana and the Reunion Island

00:13:12.250 --> 00:13:17.250
opted for and asked
for departmentalization

00:13:19.410 --> 00:13:24.330
and as a full incorporation
into the French Republic.

00:13:24.330 --> 00:13:28.060
And even if the choice can be
and the fact that they lobbied

00:13:28.060 --> 00:13:30.727
for that that they did a
proposition, we're going

00:13:30.727 --> 00:13:35.010
in that direction was
then considered to be sort

00:13:35.010 --> 00:13:39.083
of a misguided and a wrong decision.

00:13:39.930 --> 00:13:42.810
When contextualized, it was actually

00:13:42.810 --> 00:13:45.460
a pretty pragmatic choice

00:13:45.460 --> 00:13:48.150
and that's how they've been defending it.

00:13:48.150 --> 00:13:50.490
and above all, it's
important to consider it,

00:13:50.490 --> 00:13:54.250
and it's something that local
historians have been showing

00:13:54.250 --> 00:13:59.250
as the results of a long
struggle for equality driven

00:14:00.650 --> 00:14:04.770
by a number of calculations
and a strong sense

00:14:04.770 --> 00:14:09.510
of history since the
abolition of slavery in 1848.

00:14:10.360 --> 00:14:15.260
And basically this moment
of departmentalization

00:14:15.260 --> 00:14:19.380
was considered to be the sort
of promise of overturning

00:14:19.380 --> 00:14:23.160
the colonial relationship
between metropolitan France

00:14:23.160 --> 00:14:26.210
and its old colonies,
and (indistinct) colony.

00:14:26.210 --> 00:14:31.210
And it was seen as an important moment

00:14:32.610 --> 00:14:35.130
for completing equality,

00:14:35.130 --> 00:14:39.370
for repairing emancipation's failures.

00:14:39.370 --> 00:14:41.920
And if there was a true belief

00:14:41.920 --> 00:14:45.230
at the time that
departmentalization would be so,

00:14:45.230 --> 00:14:48.191
would allow for true equality

00:14:48.191 --> 00:14:52.380
with all French citizens
within the framework

00:14:52.380 --> 00:14:56.300
of the French Republic
political practices at the time,

00:14:56.300 --> 00:14:59.260
the forms of exercising power

00:14:59.260 --> 00:15:04.260
on parts of high civil servants,
the (indistinct) locally,

00:15:04.640 --> 00:15:09.130
the very partial extension of
social and economic rights,

00:15:09.130 --> 00:15:13.800
the strong repression
of political movements,

00:15:13.800 --> 00:15:17.920
all of this fed the very strong criticism

00:15:17.920 --> 00:15:19.470
of the departmentalization process,

00:15:19.470 --> 00:15:21.820
and just a few years after it was voted,

00:15:21.820 --> 00:15:26.820
it was actually actually
considered by many as a failure

00:15:27.140 --> 00:15:31.910
and a set of lies on part
of the French Republic

00:15:31.910 --> 00:15:36.910
to actually rent a real
equality and emancipation.

00:15:37.690 --> 00:15:41.230
And this criticism, the
wide array of criticisms

00:15:41.230 --> 00:15:44.030
against departmentalization process

00:15:44.030 --> 00:15:48.590
became a very important full
(indistinct) in local politics.

00:15:48.590 --> 00:15:52.150
It fed nationalism, it fed the desire

00:15:52.150 --> 00:15:55.890
for independence or political
autonomy or a wide array

00:15:55.890 --> 00:15:59.600
of political imagination
regarding what to make

00:15:59.600 --> 00:16:01.970
of these territories.

00:16:01.970 --> 00:16:06.970
It guided many people in the
military and political sphere

00:16:07.030 --> 00:16:12.030
in the way they would think
about a true political, social

00:16:12.480 --> 00:16:15.290
and cultural emancipation,
and actually Zaka could talk

00:16:15.290 --> 00:16:17.880
about it, about true economic
emancipation as well.

00:16:17.880 --> 00:16:21.660
People, because of the false promises

00:16:21.660 --> 00:16:25.470
of departmentalization,
started to think about all

00:16:25.470 --> 00:16:29.610
of that creative stuff, failed,
succeeded in some extent,

00:16:29.610 --> 00:16:33.810
and all of that history
and really does a lot

00:16:33.810 --> 00:16:36.250
of work that remains to be done

00:16:36.250 --> 00:16:39.800
on all of these political,
social, cultural experiences.

00:16:39.800 --> 00:16:44.800
Zaka's work is probably, it's
clearly part of this effort

00:16:45.880 --> 00:16:49.590
that we can see nowadays in
scholarship to feel the gaps

00:16:49.590 --> 00:16:53.070
of the contemporary history of Martinique.

00:16:53.070 --> 00:16:57.590
I'm thinking about the work
of (indistinct) as well

00:16:57.590 --> 00:17:02.200
who's a PhD candidate in
history of Martinique as well.

00:17:02.200 --> 00:17:05.270
But all of these moments that still need

00:17:05.270 --> 00:17:10.270
to be scholarly documented
are key to understand the way

00:17:14.625 --> 00:17:17.980
a number of people
viewed the events of 2020

00:17:17.980 --> 00:17:20.070
around commemoration, around the statutes,

00:17:20.070 --> 00:17:21.750
et cetera, et cetera.

00:17:21.750 --> 00:17:22.990
And I think it was very important.

00:17:22.990 --> 00:17:24.040
So what happened exactly?

00:17:24.040 --> 00:17:27.047
You mentioned it, on May 22nd 2020,

00:17:27.047 --> 00:17:29.290
it was the day of commemoration
of the insurrection

00:17:29.290 --> 00:17:32.710
of the slaves in 1848, and it's the year

00:17:32.710 --> 00:17:35.990
of the abolition of slavery
in the French colonies.

00:17:35.990 --> 00:17:38.580
And we learned, I was in
Paris where I've been living

00:17:38.580 --> 00:17:41.580
for 22 years, just like
you, I had to, I left

00:17:41.580 --> 00:17:44.080
after the (speaking in foreign language)

00:17:44.080 --> 00:17:46.540
Even if I try to go back
often and then I work

00:17:46.540 --> 00:17:50.990
on the Martinique politic and society.

00:17:50.990 --> 00:17:52.620
But we learned the destruction

00:17:52.620 --> 00:17:56.200
of our two statutes of Victor
Schoelcher in Martinique.

00:17:56.200 --> 00:17:58.720
Most people didn't cry
over these two statues,

00:17:58.720 --> 00:18:00.920
and a lot of people didn't totally care,

00:18:00.920 --> 00:18:03.740
but a lot of them didn't quite
understand what was going on.

00:18:03.740 --> 00:18:08.720
And at that moment I wondered,
I was like, it's May 22nd.

00:18:08.720 --> 00:18:10.490
I'm not supposed to have construction work

00:18:10.490 --> 00:18:14.174
on that day, it's (faintly speaking) day.

00:18:14.174 --> 00:18:18.470
And I did not totally understand
what was going on at first.

00:18:18.470 --> 00:18:20.700
The day had been marked
by lots of commemorations

00:18:20.700 --> 00:18:23.270
in several places that
had been transforming

00:18:23.270 --> 00:18:26.889
to places of memory, such as (indistinct)

00:18:26.889 --> 00:18:29.670
Lots of strong events
actually up that day.

00:18:29.670 --> 00:18:30.870
And then there was that.

00:18:32.870 --> 00:18:37.120
A few days after, actually
more than a months after that,

00:18:37.120 --> 00:18:40.150
the statue of (indistinct)
was also imported.

00:18:40.150 --> 00:18:42.790
He was a politician at the
end of the 19th century.

00:18:42.790 --> 00:18:44.470
He belonged to the social group

00:18:44.470 --> 00:18:47.140
of the (speaking in foreign language)

00:18:47.140 --> 00:18:48.770
Became a representative.

00:18:48.770 --> 00:18:51.800
He was the President of the
General Council of Martinique.

00:18:51.800 --> 00:18:54.267
Most people in Martinique have
no idea who he is exactly.

00:18:54.267 --> 00:18:55.393
And as the post is the name of a street

00:18:55.393 --> 00:19:00.200
in Martinique and people don't
really know much about him.

00:19:00.200 --> 00:19:03.230
There was no direct claim
or real debate on the figure

00:19:03.230 --> 00:19:07.560
of the man who was not
totaled really well known

00:19:08.890 --> 00:19:13.360
and people don't really know
what to make of that event.

00:19:13.360 --> 00:19:15.520
And then at the end of July, the statues

00:19:15.520 --> 00:19:17.590
of d'Esnambuc and Josephine de Beauharnais

00:19:17.590 --> 00:19:22.590
were also destroyed by the
same group, apparently.

00:19:22.670 --> 00:19:27.670
And in that case, as you said,
in the case of Josephine,

00:19:30.980 --> 00:19:33.230
her statues had already been the site

00:19:33.230 --> 00:19:35.750
of various degradations of our history,

00:19:35.750 --> 00:19:38.660
and the most important one
being that she had been beheaded

00:19:38.660 --> 00:19:39.960
at the beginning of the '90s,

00:19:39.960 --> 00:19:42.880
which was a very impressive mark.

00:19:42.880 --> 00:19:45.440
I remember it when I was little, probably

00:19:45.440 --> 00:19:48.740
at the time when we met,
the beginning of the '90s,

00:19:48.740 --> 00:19:52.890
and it was actually a very
powerful thing to see,

00:19:52.890 --> 00:19:54.670
to walk by as these statues

00:19:54.670 --> 00:19:57.300
without a head and with all kind

00:19:57.300 --> 00:19:59.903
of degradations and
transformations over time.

00:20:01.460 --> 00:20:04.420
And so even though people would
not cry over this colonial,

00:20:04.420 --> 00:20:07.110
there was no colonial
nostalgia on the part

00:20:07.110 --> 00:20:10.360
of any Martiniquan regarding
all of the symbols.

00:20:10.360 --> 00:20:13.910
There was some level of misunderstanding,

00:20:13.910 --> 00:20:16.660
but also of understanding that
the situation of Martinique

00:20:16.660 --> 00:20:18.900
is generally pretty bad in terms

00:20:18.900 --> 00:20:21.660
of unemployment, in terms
of the sanitary crisis

00:20:21.660 --> 00:20:24.910
and the health crisis and the
scandal of the chlordecone,

00:20:24.910 --> 00:20:26.690
and so people have lots of reasons.

00:20:26.690 --> 00:20:28.050
It's something that we were discussing

00:20:28.050 --> 00:20:29.730
when preparing the session.

00:20:29.730 --> 00:20:34.100
Now like 1,000 reasons to
burn down a lot in Martinique

00:20:34.100 --> 00:20:36.340
today at the contemporary era.

00:20:36.340 --> 00:20:40.400
This specific actions were
not totally understood

00:20:40.400 --> 00:20:45.400
because precisely of this
history of interventions

00:20:46.270 --> 00:20:51.270
of people from Martinique
on their own urban setting,

00:20:51.400 --> 00:20:54.410
I think that Zaka is
going to talk about that.

00:20:54.410 --> 00:20:57.203
And in the media comments
regarding this events,

00:20:59.430 --> 00:21:03.690
the distractions were attributed
to several generic actors,

00:21:03.690 --> 00:21:06.920
the revolted youths, the activists

00:21:06.920 --> 00:21:11.830
of the (indistinct) movement,
the people of Martinique,

00:21:11.830 --> 00:21:16.167
the people is also a category
that's been often mobilized.

00:21:19.030 --> 00:21:22.340
And it was interesting to kind of try

00:21:22.340 --> 00:21:25.740
and understand who was behind these evils,

00:21:25.740 --> 00:21:28.000
but to understand that behind this event,

00:21:28.000 --> 00:21:31.580
you had those some
militants that were known

00:21:31.580 --> 00:21:34.988
in Martinique as because
they intervened (indistinct)

00:21:34.988 --> 00:21:36.780
intervened a lot on social media,

00:21:36.780 --> 00:21:41.687
and are presenting themselves
as anti-chlordecone activists.

00:21:42.970 --> 00:21:45.340
So there was this connection
that was established

00:21:45.340 --> 00:21:49.350
between them and the
anti-chlordecone movement.

00:21:49.350 --> 00:21:51.253
That was some connections as well

00:21:51.253 --> 00:21:56.253
with the notion of reparation
in the public sphere.

00:21:57.610 --> 00:21:59.660
But there was clearly an indication

00:21:59.660 --> 00:22:04.660
of a general problem regarding
the issue of reparation,

00:22:05.380 --> 00:22:06.360
the issue of the health scandal

00:22:06.360 --> 00:22:08.420
of chlordecone without
really understanding

00:22:08.420 --> 00:22:13.420
whether the (indistinct)
action was totally accurate

00:22:13.890 --> 00:22:18.620
or whether this was, there was difficulty

00:22:18.620 --> 00:22:20.823
to understand the whole thing.

00:22:21.980 --> 00:22:25.830
What was interesting was the reactions

00:22:25.830 --> 00:22:30.080
to the event.

00:22:30.080 --> 00:22:32.350
From the outset, a number
of politicians condemned

00:22:32.350 --> 00:22:34.950
the destruction of the
statues of Victor Schoelcher,

00:22:35.810 --> 00:22:37.700
and the (indistinct) reaction was

00:22:37.700 --> 00:22:39.950
of the Mayor of Fort-de-France's
reaction was basically

00:22:39.950 --> 00:22:42.140
to speed up the setting
up of the commission

00:22:42.140 --> 00:22:43.840
that he had already planned

00:22:43.840 --> 00:22:46.540
on having on memory and transmission,

00:22:46.540 --> 00:22:48.610
and in which for full
disclosure I was asked

00:22:48.610 --> 00:22:52.980
to sit in, Zaka is also
part of the commission.

00:22:52.980 --> 00:22:55.340
The commission brings together
academics, professors,

00:22:55.340 --> 00:22:59.900
associative activists
and wide array of persons

00:22:59.900 --> 00:23:02.480
of several generations to offer space

00:23:02.480 --> 00:23:06.270
for discussion on these issues
with the idea of setting up

00:23:06.270 --> 00:23:08.440
short, medium and long-term actions

00:23:08.440 --> 00:23:10.960
regarding these questions.

00:23:10.960 --> 00:23:14.520
One of the very immediate
action that was organized

00:23:14.520 --> 00:23:17.530
was the setting up of (indistinct)

00:23:17.530 --> 00:23:20.360
which was a sort of public
arena in which the public

00:23:20.360 --> 00:23:23.400
and people and including
some of the people

00:23:23.400 --> 00:23:25.520
within the movement that was claiming

00:23:26.457 --> 00:23:29.400
the onboarding of the statues

00:23:29.400 --> 00:23:32.810
to (indistinct) in the open air in July

00:23:32.810 --> 00:23:35.960
in Fort-de-France to debate
on the issues of statues

00:23:35.960 --> 00:23:37.270
in the public space.

00:23:37.270 --> 00:23:41.390
And as I was telling you guys yesterday,

00:23:41.390 --> 00:23:46.090
it was very interesting
and impressive to see

00:23:46.090 --> 00:23:50.380
how almost immediately, while
the events were unfolding,

00:23:50.380 --> 00:23:52.410
like this capacity to
actually discuss about them,

00:23:52.410 --> 00:23:55.573
to talk about all of the
problems, issues, contradictions,

00:23:55.573 --> 00:23:58.730
having old politicians, younger activists

00:23:58.730 --> 00:24:02.770
actually exchanging the
issue and being able

00:24:02.770 --> 00:24:07.630
to have a sort of reflexive
notion on this, each defending

00:24:07.630 --> 00:24:11.410
his way to do politics and
each defending his way to look

00:24:11.410 --> 00:24:16.410
at the event and reflecting
on recent history

00:24:19.140 --> 00:24:19.973
and longer history.

00:24:19.973 --> 00:24:22.800
It was a very interesting
and important moment

00:24:22.800 --> 00:24:26.520
that to me has to be placed
within the long series

00:24:26.520 --> 00:24:30.740
of experiences formulated by people

00:24:30.740 --> 00:24:33.070
in Martinique since the
departmentalization to try

00:24:33.070 --> 00:24:38.063
and think about ways to
emancipate themselves.

00:24:38.900 --> 00:24:42.440
What's interesting is, as you
said in your introduction,

00:24:42.440 --> 00:24:45.650
one of the issues, and I
think that I'm almost reached

00:24:45.650 --> 00:24:50.650
my 10 minutes, I think that
one of the interesting issues

00:24:51.240 --> 00:24:53.720
is that we have all of that in Martinique,

00:24:53.720 --> 00:24:56.120
people able to think about things,

00:24:56.120 --> 00:24:58.860
people who have been imagining futures

00:24:58.860 --> 00:25:01.980
and emancipate futures for Martinique

00:25:01.980 --> 00:25:05.090
and at the same time, this
sort of high technicization

00:25:05.090 --> 00:25:08.580
of political issues that
to me constitute part

00:25:08.580 --> 00:25:10.540
of the problems in Martinique
that we are discussing

00:25:10.540 --> 00:25:12.810
whether this article of
the constitution is better

00:25:12.810 --> 00:25:14.590
than this other one article
of the constitution,

00:25:14.590 --> 00:25:17.840
without having political
actors able to formulate

00:25:17.840 --> 00:25:20.760
at a broader level a sort of
political project that people

00:25:20.760 --> 00:25:21.810
can all embrace.

00:25:21.810 --> 00:25:23.930
And I'm sorry, I concluded really rapidly,

00:25:23.930 --> 00:25:26.453
but I'll let Zaka continue (laughing)

00:25:26.453 --> 00:25:27.286
Thank you.

00:25:30.716 --> 00:25:31.910
- Thank you very much.

00:25:31.910 --> 00:25:33.953
Zaka, let's hear from you.

00:25:36.160 --> 00:25:36.993
- Hello everyone.

00:25:36.993 --> 00:25:39.590
How do I share my screen?

00:25:39.590 --> 00:25:42.410
I did a small presentation,

00:25:42.410 --> 00:25:44.083
basically just pictures.

00:25:46.900 --> 00:25:48.260
How do I activate the?

00:25:48.260 --> 00:25:50.120
Oh, yeah, share screen, there you go.

00:25:50.120 --> 00:25:50.953
Up, up, up.

00:25:50.953 --> 00:25:54.040
This phone, share.

00:25:54.040 --> 00:25:55.240
Can you see the picture?

00:25:56.110 --> 00:25:56.943
Sorry.

00:25:56.943 --> 00:25:57.776
Okay.

00:25:59.710 --> 00:26:03.330
So I've prepared some things.

00:26:03.330 --> 00:26:04.930
So hi, my name is Zaka.

00:26:04.930 --> 00:26:07.160
Thank you for the introduction (laughing)

00:26:07.160 --> 00:26:07.993
Let me say.

00:26:10.490 --> 00:26:12.080
First of all, I would like to be clear

00:26:12.080 --> 00:26:13.173
about who's talking,

00:26:14.760 --> 00:26:18.450
because often in these kind of talks

00:26:18.450 --> 00:26:22.507
about when we have experts
and historians and people

00:26:22.507 --> 00:26:24.600
who write about subjects,

00:26:24.600 --> 00:26:27.310
we tend to put in one
category people who act

00:26:27.310 --> 00:26:31.710
and people who think, and I
think it's very troubling.

00:26:31.710 --> 00:26:34.190
It's very troubling in
the black perspective

00:26:34.190 --> 00:26:37.070
because I don't think
at some point in history

00:26:37.070 --> 00:26:37.903
there was a difference

00:26:37.903 --> 00:26:40.830
between a black activist
and black intellectuals.

00:26:40.830 --> 00:26:43.790
This is in pretty much
all parts of the diaspora,

00:26:43.790 --> 00:26:46.110
something that is very linked.

00:26:46.110 --> 00:26:48.320
And I believe that in
my case, it's also kind

00:26:48.320 --> 00:26:53.320
of a dichotomy or alliance
or I don't know, a problem.

00:26:55.990 --> 00:26:57.240
I would also to add

00:26:57.240 --> 00:27:00.870
that I really don't care
about the divided fate

00:27:00.870 --> 00:27:03.850
of the statue in, the
(indistinct) and did not fit

00:27:08.700 --> 00:27:09.533
with my ideology.

00:27:09.533 --> 00:27:12.260
I identify myself as an independentist

00:27:12.260 --> 00:27:14.020
basically since I was born.

00:27:14.020 --> 00:27:19.020
I was born in despite I for me Schoelcher

00:27:19.530 --> 00:27:22.520
or whatever are historical figures.

00:27:22.520 --> 00:27:26.390
They can be important in some,
or related to my history,

00:27:26.390 --> 00:27:29.850
but I don't feel like they belong more

00:27:29.850 --> 00:27:34.253
than people coming from
actually the enslaved parts.

00:27:36.820 --> 00:27:38.950
Violence can be legitimate,

00:27:38.950 --> 00:27:42.133
but I would rather say
violence can be pertinent.

00:27:43.570 --> 00:27:46.730
And to further to fight, I'm 36,

00:27:46.730 --> 00:27:48.620
so I feel like I'm still a bit young,

00:27:48.620 --> 00:27:51.160
and I don't wanna do
what was done before me.

00:27:51.160 --> 00:27:55.170
And so for that, if I support, I say, hey,

00:27:55.170 --> 00:27:57.100
how do I further the fight?

00:27:57.100 --> 00:28:00.480
Then for me, what I studied

00:28:00.480 --> 00:28:02.740
is kind of a benchmark, what was done

00:28:02.740 --> 00:28:05.273
before me and what I can do after that.

00:28:07.200 --> 00:28:11.810
And we have the resources to
actually think about that.

00:28:11.810 --> 00:28:14.470
So that's why I wrote
a little presentation

00:28:14.470 --> 00:28:17.480
called Must Fall, Already Happened,

00:28:17.480 --> 00:28:19.453
Never Truly Ends, and Then What?

00:28:20.620 --> 00:28:22.033
I'm gonna be provocative,

00:28:23.100 --> 00:28:25.920
but it's interesting to
go across the diaspora.

00:28:25.920 --> 00:28:29.440
It's interesting to go
deep into our history

00:28:29.440 --> 00:28:32.830
and see what was done, our
echoes across communities

00:28:32.830 --> 00:28:35.640
in the diaspora and
some of the lessons also

00:28:35.640 --> 00:28:38.233
that can be learned from
some of those experiences.

00:28:39.400 --> 00:28:42.500
So of course, I believe
mostly American audience

00:28:42.500 --> 00:28:44.890
and pretty much all of
the audience, I've heard

00:28:44.890 --> 00:28:48.150
about the Charlottesville
protest in 2017 August,

00:28:48.150 --> 00:28:52.670
where basically there
was a right-wing protest

00:28:52.670 --> 00:28:54.440
to protect a statue of General Lee.

00:28:54.440 --> 00:28:56.490
I think that's the statue of General Lee,

00:28:57.540 --> 00:28:59.950
who was basically the
general leading the army

00:29:02.770 --> 00:29:05.330
to maintain the institution of slavery,

00:29:05.330 --> 00:29:09.373
if I use their language, the
language of the confederacy.

00:29:11.030 --> 00:29:12.027
And there was a counter-protest

00:29:12.027 --> 00:29:16.940
and a young woman died, I
forgot her name, I'm sorry.

00:29:16.940 --> 00:29:19.790
And this part, global conversation

00:29:19.790 --> 00:29:23.930
about the issue of colonial
legacy is very, very pertinent

00:29:23.930 --> 00:29:25.870
in the U.S., but it's
pertinent pretty much anywhere

00:29:25.870 --> 00:29:28.840
where there was an empire
and there were colonies.

00:29:28.840 --> 00:29:32.290
I think it's everywhere, Asia, Africa

00:29:32.290 --> 00:29:34.820
and Europe itself,
because Europe celebrates

00:29:34.820 --> 00:29:39.820
its own conquerors, admirals,
merchants, heroes, pirates.

00:29:43.330 --> 00:29:48.330
And at that time, in August,
that sparked something in me

00:29:48.910 --> 00:29:53.520
because when I was a kid, so
1991, I was seven years old,

00:29:53.520 --> 00:29:58.520
and I knew some of the
people, this is another,

00:29:59.400 --> 00:30:01.446
I think this is the Arlington Memorial

00:30:01.446 --> 00:30:05.490
in Virginia as well,

00:30:05.490 --> 00:30:08.200
with the mommy crying for
the confederate soldier.

00:30:08.200 --> 00:30:09.850
And this happened.

00:30:09.850 --> 00:30:12.983
So 1991, the statue of
Josephine was beheaded,

00:30:13.910 --> 00:30:15.260
and blood was poured on it.

00:30:16.344 --> 00:30:20.210
And those guys were my
heroes because basically

00:30:20.210 --> 00:30:24.560
in the '80s where it was the height

00:30:24.560 --> 00:30:27.380
and the peak of the last
really violent fights

00:30:27.380 --> 00:30:29.523
in the Caribbean, in French Guadeloupe,

00:30:31.078 --> 00:30:34.680
in Martinique and in French Guiana,

00:30:34.680 --> 00:30:36.610
it was basically almost the last wave

00:30:36.610 --> 00:30:40.433
of Caribbean independence,
which was not fruitful.

00:30:41.641 --> 00:30:45.616
And there was bombings and fights

00:30:45.616 --> 00:30:49.110
and militancy and people
were hiding weapons

00:30:49.110 --> 00:30:50.830
and it was happening everywhere.

00:30:50.830 --> 00:30:53.357
There was bombing in Paris,
there was bombing in Martinique

00:30:53.357 --> 00:30:56.483
and Guadeloupe, like almost
like 10, 20 times a year.

00:30:57.490 --> 00:30:58.993
And that was very serious.

00:30:59.830 --> 00:31:01.490
Of course it didn't pan out.

00:31:01.490 --> 00:31:03.180
It was not successful.

00:31:03.180 --> 00:31:07.660
And what happened was
some of the militants,

00:31:07.660 --> 00:31:12.660
at least some of the sales
that were in Martinique decided

00:31:13.050 --> 00:31:18.023
to go and and see the statue of Josephine.

00:31:18.023 --> 00:31:21.700
So (indistinct) Josephine
of France was married

00:31:21.700 --> 00:31:25.320
to Napoleon, and beheaded
and pour blood on it.

00:31:25.320 --> 00:31:28.683
And this statue is a paradox,

00:31:29.840 --> 00:31:33.130
because at the same time, you're saying

00:31:33.130 --> 00:31:37.640
that it's over, this kind of heritage,

00:31:37.640 --> 00:31:40.135
this colonial heritage,
because this statute

00:31:40.135 --> 00:31:45.135
was basically erected in the
I think mid 19th century,

00:31:45.420 --> 00:31:49.700
to celebrate the union
between the French Empire,

00:31:49.700 --> 00:31:52.370
I think it was the second
empire at the time,

00:31:52.370 --> 00:31:55.790
and between France and Martinique,

00:31:55.790 --> 00:31:57.920
and how this bond was materialized

00:31:57.920 --> 00:32:02.610
by Josephine who was born a
white planter in Martinique,

00:32:02.610 --> 00:32:04.460
but married to a French emperor,

00:32:04.460 --> 00:32:07.030
so how that bond was there forever.

00:32:07.030 --> 00:32:09.690
This statue was located in
the center of Fort-de-France,

00:32:09.690 --> 00:32:13.100
so the main city, and at
the center of the Savannah,

00:32:13.100 --> 00:32:16.113
which is the basically central park.

00:32:18.028 --> 00:32:21.160
Actually those two places
have the same function

00:32:21.160 --> 00:32:24.063
in history in the new
cities of the new world.

00:32:26.015 --> 00:32:29.450
And to beheaded it was a way to say,

00:32:29.450 --> 00:32:33.350
this thing and the way it was established

00:32:33.350 --> 00:32:35.363
and the way it was symbolized is over.

00:32:36.390 --> 00:32:41.390
But at the same time, it
was also a symbolic act

00:32:42.400 --> 00:32:45.090
because the military action
and the (indistinct) action

00:32:45.090 --> 00:32:47.973
and the political action
was not successful.

00:32:49.280 --> 00:32:54.280
So it's symbolic with, but in concrete

00:32:54.360 --> 00:32:58.180
maybe it is not (faintly speaking)

00:33:00.052 --> 00:33:02.080
And it kind of reflects very well

00:33:02.080 --> 00:33:03.470
what happened in Martinique.

00:33:03.470 --> 00:33:04.730
So we'll go back a little bit,

00:33:04.730 --> 00:33:07.790
and we will talk about the
history of Fort-de-France,

00:33:07.790 --> 00:33:10.520
which was basically a colonial city

00:33:10.520 --> 00:33:15.520
and the seat of power of
all the colonial powers,

00:33:15.690 --> 00:33:18.850
of all the elements of
colonial power in Martinique

00:33:18.850 --> 00:33:21.760
as in the French government
when it was a colony

00:33:22.974 --> 00:33:25.100
and still a little bit,
of the white planters

00:33:25.100 --> 00:33:29.830
called the big key of
the form of free slave

00:33:29.830 --> 00:33:33.680
or free of colors or
people who became wealthy

00:33:33.680 --> 00:33:38.150
after the abolition and
basically were kind of pro-France

00:33:38.150 --> 00:33:43.150
and pro-assimilation, and how this gap

00:33:43.160 --> 00:33:48.160
twisted basically after World
War II, thanks to two things.

00:33:51.320 --> 00:33:53.150
First, there was people coming

00:33:53.150 --> 00:33:56.930
from the countryside who
moved massively to the city.

00:33:56.930 --> 00:33:59.780
So that changed the
demographics of the city.

00:33:59.780 --> 00:34:04.170
People who were voting were
not assimilating themselves

00:34:04.170 --> 00:34:07.750
as the winners of colonization,

00:34:07.750 --> 00:34:12.750
they were the former forced
laborers, exploited laborer,

00:34:14.460 --> 00:34:16.310
and basically they could finally express

00:34:16.310 --> 00:34:20.040
some political power, and meeting

00:34:20.040 --> 00:34:25.040
with multiple generation of
intellectuals and writers.

00:34:26.130 --> 00:34:31.130
So (indistinct) were firstly
anti-colonialist, and.

00:34:33.937 --> 00:34:36.320
(faintly speaking)

00:34:36.320 --> 00:34:41.320
And basically made a political
offer to take the reins

00:34:41.600 --> 00:34:43.730
of the capital and transform it.

00:34:43.730 --> 00:34:44.800
And this is what happened,

00:34:44.800 --> 00:34:48.780
because so the movement
was called the Negritude.

00:34:48.780 --> 00:34:51.080
They were claiming
themselves to be cannibals

00:34:51.080 --> 00:34:55.500
and they actually eat all, a
lot of the political power,

00:34:55.500 --> 00:34:58.670
a lot of those colonial
legacy from the inside

00:34:58.670 --> 00:35:01.010
and from the outside,
they change the function

00:35:01.010 --> 00:35:02.760
of some places.

00:35:02.760 --> 00:35:05.040
So here for example on this picture,

00:35:05.040 --> 00:35:07.760
you have these gates, I think in English

00:35:07.760 --> 00:35:09.750
it's called (speaking in
foreign language) in French

00:35:09.750 --> 00:35:14.750
and twin centenary gates, I think, whoop!

00:35:15.380 --> 00:35:16.220
Oh, okay.

00:35:16.220 --> 00:35:17.070
Good translation.

00:35:19.200 --> 00:35:21.590
Which was erected in 1935

00:35:22.610 --> 00:35:24.873
because as Cecile mentioned earlier,

00:35:26.310 --> 00:35:29.260
the Colony of Martinique
was established in 1635.

00:35:29.260 --> 00:35:32.900
So it was to celebrate
300 years of the empire

00:35:32.900 --> 00:35:37.003
and 300 years of where the empire started.

00:35:37.855 --> 00:35:38.688
(faintly speaking)

00:35:38.688 --> 00:35:39.559
Okay.

00:35:39.559 --> 00:35:43.990
And basically what happened
is that Cesaire was mayor

00:35:43.990 --> 00:35:48.990
for 50 years, transformed these
gates, not by coupling it,

00:35:49.440 --> 00:35:53.970
not by destroying it, but
by changing what was inside.

00:35:53.970 --> 00:35:58.970
What was inside was basically
a Center for Cultural Actions

00:35:59.570 --> 00:36:04.570
that was to celebrate black
arts, bringing the diasporas,

00:36:04.730 --> 00:36:08.730
teach people culture, art
and all kinds of things.

00:36:08.730 --> 00:36:10.750
And actually a lot of
very prestigious things

00:36:10.750 --> 00:36:12.420
were done there, I cannot go into details,

00:36:12.420 --> 00:36:14.300
I really only have one minute.

00:36:14.300 --> 00:36:17.600
And there was just, you
can see at the basement,

00:36:17.600 --> 00:36:19.870
one modification that was made.

00:36:19.870 --> 00:36:21.750
There was a fresco that
was done in the '80s,

00:36:21.750 --> 00:36:24.530
early '80s by an artist
called (indistinct)

00:36:24.530 --> 00:36:27.050
And basically that
celebrates the insurrection

00:36:27.050 --> 00:36:29.060
of the Caribbean engines.

00:36:29.060 --> 00:36:30.630
So there was this also symbol

00:36:30.630 --> 00:36:34.343
equivalent so of what this gate meant.

00:36:36.340 --> 00:36:39.460
So change the function, so
that's a political action,

00:36:39.460 --> 00:36:41.080
a real concrete action.

00:36:41.080 --> 00:36:44.833
And then a simple
transformation of the place.

00:36:45.830 --> 00:36:47.560
Just one conclusion, I
will not have the time

00:36:47.560 --> 00:36:49.400
to go through Algeria and what happened

00:36:49.400 --> 00:36:51.380
to the Stroessner Statue in Paraguay.

00:36:51.380 --> 00:36:54.650
Basically they keep some of
those symbols as reminders

00:36:54.650 --> 00:36:57.280
of what happened before
rather than erase them

00:36:57.280 --> 00:36:59.210
as if they will never come back again,

00:36:59.210 --> 00:37:02.800
because in democracy or in culture

00:37:02.800 --> 00:37:06.713
or in human societies,
things tend to come back.

00:37:07.700 --> 00:37:10.090
And I choose this last symbol,

00:37:10.090 --> 00:37:12.790
which is a boulevard in the U.S.

00:37:12.790 --> 00:37:14.830
called Martin Luther King Boulevard.

00:37:14.830 --> 00:37:18.080
And I remember when I was 20 years old,

00:37:18.080 --> 00:37:20.530
I was reading this comic
called "The Boondocks",

00:37:20.530 --> 00:37:22.764
and one of the characters of comics

00:37:22.764 --> 00:37:27.560
was pointing out that some
of the most dangerous places

00:37:27.560 --> 00:37:29.710
and avenues in the United States

00:37:29.710 --> 00:37:32.940
are places called Martin
Luther King Boulevard.

00:37:32.940 --> 00:37:36.120
And when I was a teenager
in the small city,

00:37:36.120 --> 00:37:37.990
I'm from the countryside of Martinique,

00:37:37.990 --> 00:37:39.040
there was this place named

00:37:39.040 --> 00:37:43.480
after the revered famous
journalist in (indistinct)

00:37:43.480 --> 00:37:46.710
And this place turned out with the people

00:37:46.710 --> 00:37:48.310
from my age, people I went to school with,

00:37:48.310 --> 00:37:49.540
and then the younger people after me,

00:37:49.540 --> 00:37:54.540
it was basically a dope-selling area.

00:37:56.690 --> 00:37:58.920
And I remember, at some points,

00:37:58.920 --> 00:38:03.423
military was sent to clean that street,

00:38:04.630 --> 00:38:05.540
but the name was there.

00:38:05.540 --> 00:38:08.670
The change of names were there,
the symbols were changed,

00:38:08.670 --> 00:38:11.260
but the concrete, what
was happening at that time

00:38:11.260 --> 00:38:14.240
in that city despite the
nice name of the street

00:38:14.240 --> 00:38:16.707
and the fact that we reminisce
of our own black history

00:38:16.707 --> 00:38:20.300
and of our fights and of
everything, it was not good.

00:38:20.300 --> 00:38:21.890
It was not good.

00:38:21.890 --> 00:38:26.027
And I think that it's very
interesting to understand that

00:38:26.027 --> 00:38:29.083
and that misfall is not the end.

00:38:34.700 --> 00:38:36.170
- Thank you very much Zaka.

00:38:39.560 --> 00:38:43.240
So I think we can come together now

00:38:43.240 --> 00:38:47.063
for a little bit of a panel discussion.

00:38:47.950 --> 00:38:52.380
But before we start, I
would like to invite members

00:38:52.380 --> 00:38:56.020
of the audience to feel
free to send their questions

00:38:56.020 --> 00:39:01.020
starting now in the Q&amp;A function
as we continue to discuss

00:39:03.150 --> 00:39:07.017
and we will get to them very soon.

00:39:09.320 --> 00:39:10.510
So thank you for doing that,

00:39:10.510 --> 00:39:15.510
and we are definitely recording
them to get to them later.

00:39:21.140 --> 00:39:25.340
I was really struck by the ways

00:39:25.340 --> 00:39:30.340
in which we are dealing
with a very dense terrain

00:39:30.480 --> 00:39:35.017
in terms of contemporary
history in Martinique,

00:39:35.017 --> 00:39:38.990
but something that, Audrey, you mentioned

00:39:40.740 --> 00:39:45.740
in the sphere of political
action and Zaka also relating it

00:39:48.330 --> 00:39:52.260
with the urban space and the ways

00:39:56.040 --> 00:39:59.642
in which is been resignified over time.

00:39:59.642 --> 00:40:04.122
And so a question I had for
you, something I would like us

00:40:04.122 --> 00:40:08.010
to discuss is the ways
in which the many layers

00:40:08.010 --> 00:40:13.010
of political actions and
monumental marking of space

00:40:20.990 --> 00:40:25.800
have been not always
meeting with each other

00:40:26.716 --> 00:40:31.716
in (muffled speaking) decades
since the departmentalization.

00:40:32.490 --> 00:40:37.490
And so what do you think
has been the challenges

00:40:38.574 --> 00:40:39.817
in this regard that have been met

00:40:39.817 --> 00:40:42.370
and the challenges that I've not been met

00:40:42.370 --> 00:40:46.403
in a way that led to the summer of 2020?

00:40:48.700 --> 00:40:50.570
- Well, maybe I can start.

00:40:50.570 --> 00:40:54.920
I really think that what you're
referring to is basically

00:40:54.920 --> 00:40:58.670
one of the very important
issues for people who work

00:40:58.670 --> 00:41:01.070
on public policies of memory,

00:41:01.070 --> 00:41:04.710
or memory from a very,
I'm a political scientist,

00:41:04.710 --> 00:41:06.870
so I'm going to talk
about my field (laughing)

00:41:06.870 --> 00:41:08.520
But really this notion

00:41:08.520 --> 00:41:13.220
that, you cannot say that
in the case of Martinique,

00:41:13.220 --> 00:41:15.730
decisions by political leaders,

00:41:15.730 --> 00:41:18.730
decisions by the French
State is just going

00:41:18.730 --> 00:41:22.370
to be imposing upon the
population and that's it.

00:41:22.370 --> 00:41:25.060
A public policy in memory works

00:41:25.060 --> 00:41:27.030
in public commemorations work

00:41:27.030 --> 00:41:29.530
if they're appropriated by people.

00:41:29.530 --> 00:41:31.580
Otherwise you can say
(speaking in foreign language)

00:41:31.580 --> 00:41:35.360
You can say all kinds of things
and it's gonna come back.

00:41:35.360 --> 00:41:39.160
And what's interesting is that the example

00:41:39.160 --> 00:41:41.950
that was given by Zaka
about the Tricentennial,

00:41:41.950 --> 00:41:44.500
it's difficult, Tricentennial Gates

00:41:44.500 --> 00:41:49.010
is basically, it's interesting
because it's on two levels,

00:41:49.010 --> 00:41:54.010
it's the signs of kind of failed memory

00:41:54.220 --> 00:41:56.360
or commemorations policies.

00:41:56.360 --> 00:41:59.147
First, nobody knew that
this place was called

00:41:59.147 --> 00:42:02.658
the Tricentennial Gate apart from Zaka

00:42:02.658 --> 00:42:05.600
and a few historians from Martinique,

00:42:05.600 --> 00:42:09.960
and some historian said
that they actually walked

00:42:09.960 --> 00:42:13.459
around the city to find it, there was no,

00:42:13.459 --> 00:42:18.459
it has not been remembered
as a colonial gate.

00:42:19.050 --> 00:42:21.280
It is the entrance of the (indistinct)

00:42:21.280 --> 00:42:25.280
So to that extent, it's a
success on part of Cesaire.

00:42:25.280 --> 00:42:30.280
It's a place where the
carnival stops every year,

00:42:30.540 --> 00:42:32.830
some groups stop there and
you have the final rounds

00:42:32.830 --> 00:42:37.570
of drums before it's
time to go to a party.

00:42:37.570 --> 00:42:42.570
For others, it's a place where
people just pee in public

00:42:43.120 --> 00:42:44.983
and it smells really bad.

00:42:47.471 --> 00:42:48.304
Okay, it's a place

00:42:48.304 --> 00:42:49.950
that has all kind (laughing) of functions.

00:42:49.950 --> 00:42:54.655
Nobody remember that it was
erected for the tricentennial

00:42:54.655 --> 00:42:56.380
of the colonization of Martinique.

00:42:56.380 --> 00:42:59.270
Then the work of reappropriation is known

00:42:59.270 --> 00:43:02.676
by a little more people,
because people are more

00:43:02.676 --> 00:43:06.920
with contemporary of the
reappropriation by (indistinct)

00:43:06.920 --> 00:43:08.440
that was mentioned by Zaka.

00:43:09.744 --> 00:43:12.330
More people were there,
more people witnessed it.

00:43:12.330 --> 00:43:14.870
But clearly there's
something that is lacking.

00:43:14.870 --> 00:43:18.520
The people who wanted to destroy the gate

00:43:18.520 --> 00:43:23.190
or were contesting this
colonial symbol had no idea

00:43:23.190 --> 00:43:25.250
of this work of reappropriation.

00:43:25.250 --> 00:43:27.280
And if this work of reappropriation

00:43:27.280 --> 00:43:30.580
was not fully understood,
it means that in terms

00:43:30.580 --> 00:43:33.860
of transmission, the
transmission of our own past,

00:43:33.860 --> 00:43:35.270
there is something that is missing.

00:43:35.270 --> 00:43:38.570
And finally, during the
commission that we attended,

00:43:38.570 --> 00:43:42.750
and it's something that we both
noticed, some social workers

00:43:42.750 --> 00:43:47.750
within the Commission on
Memory and Transmission said,

00:43:47.800 --> 00:43:51.380
this is a place for
people who are homeless.

00:43:51.380 --> 00:43:54.730
A lot of them are crack
addict in Fort-de-France,

00:43:54.730 --> 00:43:58.110
and that is another of
our collective failures

00:43:58.110 --> 00:44:00.870
and another of the
structural problem that exist

00:44:00.870 --> 00:44:04.400
in Martinique, the fact that the economy,

00:44:04.400 --> 00:44:07.710
from the fact that we do
have a number of social

00:44:07.710 --> 00:44:12.590
and economic problem that are
seen, are visible in the city

00:44:12.590 --> 00:44:13.423
are part of it.

00:44:13.423 --> 00:44:16.480
But I really think that
all of these layers need

00:44:16.480 --> 00:44:20.724
to be addressed, and as I agree
100% with Zaka when he says

00:44:20.724 --> 00:44:22.472
the work has been done.

00:44:22.472 --> 00:44:26.700
The work has been done on memory, symbols,

00:44:26.700 --> 00:44:28.203
and we can do more.

00:44:29.430 --> 00:44:33.280
That's true, but the
core of the action today

00:44:33.280 --> 00:44:37.830
might not be exactly there
that sparks more discussions

00:44:37.830 --> 00:44:39.840
on, okay, but what to do exactly,

00:44:39.840 --> 00:44:43.243
but I let Zaka respond now (laughing)

00:44:45.544 --> 00:44:46.377
(Zaka laughing)

00:44:46.377 --> 00:44:48.213
- So you give, here the baby.

00:44:51.350 --> 00:44:53.393
No, you're absolutely right.

00:44:57.320 --> 00:44:59.430
There's this weird paradox.

00:44:59.430 --> 00:45:00.874
There's so many example.

00:45:00.874 --> 00:45:04.300
There was two statues of Schoelcher

00:45:04.300 --> 00:45:06.220
that were destroyed Martinique.

00:45:06.220 --> 00:45:07.120
The one in Fort-de-France

00:45:07.120 --> 00:45:09.640
was absolutely paternalistic,

00:45:12.350 --> 00:45:16.090
and once you realize what it means,

00:45:16.090 --> 00:45:19.657
it's basically Schoelcher leading

00:45:19.657 --> 00:45:22.150
a young kid.

00:45:22.150 --> 00:45:24.940
I think it's a young, yeah,

00:45:24.940 --> 00:45:28.900
it's a young kid, and
breaking with this feet

00:45:28.900 --> 00:45:30.873
the shackles of slavery.

00:45:32.417 --> 00:45:36.770
And obviously in terms
of the history of it,

00:45:36.770 --> 00:45:41.770
in terms of what it wanted to transmit

00:45:42.610 --> 00:45:47.610
about who did what for the
abolition of the statute itself

00:45:48.710 --> 00:45:52.560
was in (indistinct) and
it could be destroyed

00:45:52.560 --> 00:45:54.670
or taken care of or put somewhere else,

00:45:54.670 --> 00:45:57.150
but not in the center

00:45:57.150 --> 00:46:00.733
of Fort-de-France, or unexplained.

00:46:02.270 --> 00:46:04.100
But there was another
one in Le Marin City,

00:46:04.100 --> 00:46:06.937
which actually is named after Schoelcher.

00:46:06.937 --> 00:46:09.237
And there's just a plain
statue of Schoelcher,

00:46:10.210 --> 00:46:12.550
who can be criticized for many things,

00:46:12.550 --> 00:46:17.550
but mostly it was a man ahead of his time

00:46:17.890 --> 00:46:21.350
at the moment as a white
guy who never laid foot

00:46:21.350 --> 00:46:22.553
once in Martinique.

00:46:24.840 --> 00:46:25.673
Yeah.

00:46:26.550 --> 00:46:29.480
And so in terms of those
politics of memory,

00:46:29.480 --> 00:46:31.700
just being at the statue in
the situation of Schoelcher,

00:46:31.700 --> 00:46:33.790
there was a statue of
Schoelcher which is just him.

00:46:33.790 --> 00:46:36.610
So nothing paternalistic,
just honoring the guy,

00:46:36.610 --> 00:46:40.030
and behind it, there's also a huge fresco

00:46:40.940 --> 00:46:43.760
made also by another very famous artist,

00:46:43.760 --> 00:46:46.060
also very engaged artist, who's a friend

00:46:46.060 --> 00:46:48.770
of (indistinct) also
as well, same movement,

00:46:48.770 --> 00:46:52.440
same generation of local artists,

00:46:52.440 --> 00:46:56.000
and who's also, I think
this one is celebrating

00:46:56.000 --> 00:47:00.070
a slave insurrection, and
it's the fresco is much bigger

00:47:00.070 --> 00:47:02.380
and larger and has more colors

00:47:02.380 --> 00:47:04.920
than the statue of Schoelcher.

00:47:04.920 --> 00:47:09.760
I doubt that some of the people,

00:47:09.760 --> 00:47:11.840
some of the militants would destroy

00:47:11.840 --> 00:47:12.690
the statute of Schoelcher,

00:47:12.690 --> 00:47:15.470
actually paid attention to this fresco

00:47:15.470 --> 00:47:18.610
that was behind this small statue.

00:47:18.610 --> 00:47:20.670
I don't think they paid attention to it,

00:47:20.670 --> 00:47:23.863
but somehow at the same
time, maybe they revealed it.

00:47:24.900 --> 00:47:27.680
So those are very much paradox.

00:47:27.680 --> 00:47:29.740
And those things were then 20 years ago,

00:47:29.740 --> 00:47:32.970
30 years ago, 40 years ago, 50 years ago.

00:47:32.970 --> 00:47:36.603
So once this conversation
started this year,

00:47:37.480 --> 00:47:42.160
you like, there's a discourse
that nothing has been done,

00:47:42.160 --> 00:47:44.203
we have to first notice the problem.

00:47:45.120 --> 00:47:48.500
There's this talk about
there is no memorial

00:47:48.500 --> 00:47:53.500
for maroons and resistance
coming from the enslaved.

00:47:54.160 --> 00:47:57.460
There's actually I think
22 monuments in Martinique,

00:47:57.460 --> 00:47:59.170
almost in every city,

00:47:59.170 --> 00:48:04.170
celebrating maroons and
freedom fighters basically.

00:48:04.800 --> 00:48:07.630
You have them in roundabouts,

00:48:07.630 --> 00:48:11.230
you have them, there's a huge monuments

00:48:12.740 --> 00:48:16.200
in (indistinct) with stone statues

00:48:16.200 --> 00:48:17.710
that remembers a little bit

00:48:17.710 --> 00:48:20.430
what was on the Eastern Island,

00:48:20.430 --> 00:48:23.880
facing the ocean, to celebrate
all the people who died

00:48:23.880 --> 00:48:25.870
in the middle passage.

00:48:25.870 --> 00:48:28.260
There's so many strong

00:48:28.260 --> 00:48:31.533
and powerful celebrations of this history.

00:48:32.840 --> 00:48:37.230
And somehow, because there
is this political void,

00:48:37.230 --> 00:48:41.660
we want to fill it with politics of memory

00:48:41.660 --> 00:48:44.610
as if more and more and more memory

00:48:44.610 --> 00:48:48.450
will somehow solve all the
political problems happening

00:48:48.450 --> 00:48:49.400
in the islands.

00:48:49.400 --> 00:48:54.383
And I think that's where
there is a disconnect too.

00:48:56.400 --> 00:48:57.233
- Yes, thank you.

00:48:57.233 --> 00:49:01.670
It really strikes me that
the issue of transmission

00:49:01.670 --> 00:49:06.490
is really at the core
of of what is happening,

00:49:06.490 --> 00:49:09.010
that there has been movement.

00:49:09.010 --> 00:49:14.010
There has been initiatives of
memorialization and monuments

00:49:16.750 --> 00:49:21.750
that have been built in political
activism that has happened

00:49:21.920 --> 00:49:26.339
in the relatively recent past

00:49:26.339 --> 00:49:31.083
that are either not remembered,

00:49:32.210 --> 00:49:37.210
or have are disavowed
perhaps by a new generation

00:49:40.800 --> 00:49:45.800
of activists whose claims seem

00:49:45.870 --> 00:49:50.260
to be to some extent similar,
but whose means and modes

00:49:50.260 --> 00:49:55.260
of action are quite different
from older generations.

00:49:55.680 --> 00:49:58.540
What is your thought on that?

00:49:58.540 --> 00:50:00.980
Is that part of the issue?

00:50:00.980 --> 00:50:02.230
Part of what's happening?

00:50:03.220 --> 00:50:07.770
- No, it's funny because about two weeks

00:50:07.770 --> 00:50:12.650
after the toppling of the
statues of Schoelcher,

00:50:12.650 --> 00:50:14.860
I was turning 40, so I was like, okay,

00:50:14.860 --> 00:50:16.270
maybe it's just, I'm getting old,

00:50:16.270 --> 00:50:18.430
and I'm just becoming
like super reactionary,

00:50:18.430 --> 00:50:20.580
I'm like, oh, what are
these young people doing?

00:50:20.580 --> 00:50:22.330
But it was really, I was like, okay,

00:50:22.330 --> 00:50:25.160
that's what's happening (laughing) now.

00:50:25.160 --> 00:50:27.460
And then I talked to younger
people who were like, well,

00:50:27.460 --> 00:50:29.980
we don't really get it
totally, so I was like, okay,

00:50:29.980 --> 00:50:31.180
maybe I'm not that old,

00:50:31.180 --> 00:50:33.900
or maybe it's not just
a generational thing,

00:50:33.900 --> 00:50:37.517
but more seriously, I think it puts us

00:50:41.030 --> 00:50:43.710
in a very difficult position,

00:50:43.710 --> 00:50:46.540
first because I'm, again,
I live 8,000 kilometers

00:50:46.540 --> 00:50:49.120
from Martinique, so it's
actually not easy for me

00:50:49.120 --> 00:50:52.327
to, even if I'm a scholar from Martinique

00:50:52.327 --> 00:50:55.030
and I felt lots of passion
about what was going on

00:50:55.030 --> 00:50:57.810
in Martinique, but still I'm
not living there anymore,

00:50:57.810 --> 00:51:01.490
and that's part of the
equation and that's important.

00:51:01.490 --> 00:51:04.170
But I work on the migration
and on the fact that a lot

00:51:04.170 --> 00:51:08.430
of people have had to go
away and not come back.

00:51:08.430 --> 00:51:12.510
It's not just a matter of my
will to be exploring the world,

00:51:12.510 --> 00:51:15.350
it's also the fact that it
would have been difficult

00:51:15.350 --> 00:51:20.230
for me to have my career
if I was to go back.

00:51:20.230 --> 00:51:22.910
And so my individual history is part

00:51:22.910 --> 00:51:24.280
of the most structural history

00:51:24.280 --> 00:51:27.690
that is totally key to
understand what's going on today

00:51:27.690 --> 00:51:29.918
in Martinique, that is that we have

00:51:29.918 --> 00:51:32.340
a very big demographic problem,
that is that we experienced

00:51:32.340 --> 00:51:37.280
several decades of organized
migration with a construction

00:51:37.280 --> 00:51:39.490
of a problem of
overpopulation on the island,

00:51:39.490 --> 00:51:42.560
that was also a way to
counter the insurrection.

00:51:42.560 --> 00:51:44.990
I'm going through 30 years
of history very rapidly,

00:51:44.990 --> 00:51:46.640
but that's all part of it.

00:51:46.640 --> 00:51:48.500
And so I consider that, well, I have a say

00:51:48.500 --> 00:51:49.740
on what's going on there,

00:51:49.740 --> 00:51:52.260
because I'm also the
result of that history.

00:51:52.260 --> 00:51:55.660
And the thing is it is very difficult

00:51:55.660 --> 00:52:00.660
to be addressing the actions
of young people who mobilize

00:52:04.690 --> 00:52:07.860
in a country where it's actually
hard to be a young person,

00:52:07.860 --> 00:52:10.460
in a country where the unemployment levels

00:52:10.460 --> 00:52:15.460
for the youth is very high,
where there's a normal certainty

00:52:16.080 --> 00:52:20.580
that for all of the people
involved, and probably you guys

00:52:20.580 --> 00:52:23.150
and me who grew up in Martinique,
that we have chlordecone

00:52:23.150 --> 00:52:27.370
in our systems, that we all
know women with endometriosis

00:52:27.370 --> 00:52:30.540
in Martinique, that we
all know men who died

00:52:30.540 --> 00:52:35.120
of prostatic cancers, and
that we are all in a way,

00:52:35.120 --> 00:52:37.330
we have that in all of our families.

00:52:37.330 --> 00:52:41.100
So that's a very important
issue in that to have

00:52:41.100 --> 00:52:43.270
young people who just like great stuff

00:52:43.270 --> 00:52:46.140
because it's an unbearable
situation is of course something

00:52:46.140 --> 00:52:48.520
that is understandable.

00:52:48.520 --> 00:52:51.640
But I think it's also important

00:52:51.640 --> 00:52:55.130
to really, first our history
is largely conditioned

00:52:55.130 --> 00:53:00.110
by slavery, but it's
also 107 years of stuff

00:53:00.110 --> 00:53:02.750
that we've been doing since then.

00:53:02.750 --> 00:53:04.650
All of this stuff conditioned by slavery,

00:53:04.650 --> 00:53:09.650
but 107 years of struggles,
of victories, of failures,

00:53:09.780 --> 00:53:11.450
and I think that it's important.

00:53:11.450 --> 00:53:13.690
I work on to the (indistinct) right now.

00:53:13.690 --> 00:53:16.140
You have lots of master thesis, articles,

00:53:16.140 --> 00:53:18.140
people who did stuff from the (indistinct)

00:53:18.140 --> 00:53:20.221
there is not one academic monography

00:53:20.221 --> 00:53:22.330
on the (indistinct) right now.

00:53:22.330 --> 00:53:25.120
Lots of archives haven't been open.

00:53:25.120 --> 00:53:29.360
You have lots of gaps in our history

00:53:29.360 --> 00:53:32.750
despite these strong
efforts of local historians

00:53:32.750 --> 00:53:35.860
who are really like the
driving force behind,

00:53:35.860 --> 00:53:38.130
and sometimes independent scholars,

00:53:38.130 --> 00:53:42.370
the driving force behind
unveiling very important

00:53:42.370 --> 00:53:44.880
and key aspects of our history.

00:53:44.880 --> 00:53:48.060
And there's the political
class that at the same time

00:53:48.060 --> 00:53:49.250
it's pretty competent on a lot

00:53:49.250 --> 00:53:51.600
of very technical issues, but it's kind

00:53:51.600 --> 00:53:56.133
of unable to just propose,
that has been kind of tucked

00:53:57.100 --> 00:54:00.260
in like this sort of
highly technical discussion

00:54:00.260 --> 00:54:04.890
about decentralization without
really being able to think

00:54:04.890 --> 00:54:09.053
about a plan for Martinique
in 2030, 2040, 2050,

00:54:10.200 --> 00:54:12.470
considering the challenges that we have.

00:54:12.470 --> 00:54:17.470
And at the same time, we've a very weird

00:54:17.590 --> 00:54:19.720
and complex political party structure

00:54:19.720 --> 00:54:22.860
in our country with the
failure and the collapse

00:54:22.860 --> 00:54:25.670
of the Nationalist Movement
that were also sites of train

00:54:25.670 --> 00:54:29.150
of political training,
places where you learn stuff.

00:54:29.150 --> 00:54:30.800
When you were in a political movement

00:54:30.800 --> 00:54:33.330
in a nationalist movement
and you were driving

00:54:33.330 --> 00:54:35.690
for independence, you had to read stuff,

00:54:35.690 --> 00:54:37.510
you had to be trained to do stuff.

00:54:37.510 --> 00:54:42.510
And this place is where sites
of transmission as well,

00:54:42.590 --> 00:54:44.850
just like (indistinct) that was mentioned,

00:54:44.850 --> 00:54:47.720
the place is supposed to be the entrance

00:54:47.720 --> 00:54:51.490
of the (indistinct) cultural services

00:54:51.490 --> 00:54:52.950
of the City of Paris during years.

00:54:52.950 --> 00:54:55.240
That was a place where
people would learn stuff

00:54:55.240 --> 00:54:56.660
and get politicized.

00:54:56.660 --> 00:55:00.120
And we realized that these places

00:55:00.120 --> 00:55:04.563
in our societies have
collapsed, have disappeared,

00:55:06.028 --> 00:55:10.500
Zaka who is obsessed with
urban staff and urban settings

00:55:10.500 --> 00:55:13.920
can talk about that, but
where are those places?

00:55:13.920 --> 00:55:18.920
And probably there is
a generational thing.

00:55:19.020 --> 00:55:20.790
Either you are born in
a very militant family

00:55:20.790 --> 00:55:23.710
and you know stuff, or
you are from my generation

00:55:23.710 --> 00:55:26.063
and you went to (indistinct)
and you know stuff,

00:55:27.740 --> 00:55:29.700
but for a number of people,
they're just like, okay,

00:55:29.700 --> 00:55:32.100
I wanna know stuff, so they just follow

00:55:32.100 --> 00:55:35.300
weird YouTube channels
that say total nonsense,

00:55:35.300 --> 00:55:38.920
but have 90,000 followers
because people need to know more

00:55:38.920 --> 00:55:41.350
about their history, and it's these gaps

00:55:41.350 --> 00:55:45.610
that we are trying, willing
to fill, and I think

00:55:45.610 --> 00:55:47.960
it's key, it's actually important.

00:55:47.960 --> 00:55:49.540
I've been going all over the place, sorry.

00:55:49.540 --> 00:55:50.790
Zaka (laughing) go ahead.

00:55:54.120 --> 00:55:56.073
- No, just to further your point,

00:55:57.849 --> 00:56:01.293
there's something really
strange to happened.

00:56:02.360 --> 00:56:05.390
So recently, I was thinking
about growing pains,

00:56:05.390 --> 00:56:06.470
when you when you grew up

00:56:06.470 --> 00:56:08.970
and then suddenly you lose some eyesight

00:56:08.970 --> 00:56:11.290
or the growing pains.

00:56:11.290 --> 00:56:16.290
And in the sense of must
fall, I think Martinique,

00:56:16.330 --> 00:56:20.930
we are after must fall
for like decades now.

00:56:20.930 --> 00:56:25.930
And it's almost like a
future of what happened

00:56:27.810 --> 00:56:32.810
when you are not independent
formerly, but you do have

00:56:34.700 --> 00:56:39.110
some political autonomy,
you do have some control

00:56:39.110 --> 00:56:42.200
of the destiny, and you have achieved

00:56:42.200 --> 00:56:45.370
some progress and emancipation.

00:56:45.370 --> 00:56:46.203
Some.

00:56:49.270 --> 00:56:54.270
But there's nothing, you
don't know how to formulate

00:56:54.510 --> 00:56:59.510
the next step, because
there's maybe no discourse,

00:57:00.260 --> 00:57:03.610
there is no treaties about it,
you're just making stuff up

00:57:03.610 --> 00:57:05.310
as you go.

00:57:05.310 --> 00:57:10.310
And this idea of having
both the political action

00:57:11.650 --> 00:57:16.650
or what we see is the
progress that we want

00:57:17.140 --> 00:57:18.540
or that we can achieve

00:57:18.540 --> 00:57:23.540
at a specific time and
the ability to produce

00:57:25.220 --> 00:57:28.690
the ideas and the ambitions

00:57:28.690 --> 00:57:31.910
and the visions that can help you

00:57:31.910 --> 00:57:34.783
maybe imagine this next step.

00:57:35.620 --> 00:57:40.530
In the last three years
there was, and I think

00:57:40.530 --> 00:57:42.820
we were very good at it,
and I think that's one

00:57:42.820 --> 00:57:45.140
of the reasons why Martinique
is a very specific place.

00:57:45.140 --> 00:57:48.440
I'm sorry, I'm a homer, but
we were very good at it.

00:57:48.440 --> 00:57:52.374
We have been very good at it
at having both this action,

00:57:52.374 --> 00:57:54.675
this political action that happened

00:57:54.675 --> 00:57:57.440
after the abolition of slavery
and like how do you encroach

00:57:57.440 --> 00:57:59.260
a political power step by step?

00:57:59.260 --> 00:58:01.780
Sometimes by fight, sometimes
by political actions,

00:58:01.780 --> 00:58:05.110
sometimes by militancy, sometimes by...

00:58:05.110 --> 00:58:06.470
But there was always a discourse

00:58:06.470 --> 00:58:08.400
with it that was going along with it.

00:58:08.400 --> 00:58:10.280
There was always ideas that were produced

00:58:10.280 --> 00:58:13.830
that were going along with it,
that were basically stating,

00:58:13.830 --> 00:58:17.400
okay, we've done this,
we need to do this next.

00:58:17.400 --> 00:58:20.210
And somehow maybe because
a generation disappeared,

00:58:20.210 --> 00:58:21.670
maybe because the fabric

00:58:21.670 --> 00:58:23.923
of society itself has changed,

00:58:24.860 --> 00:58:29.860
even though in the last 50 years or so,

00:58:29.940 --> 00:58:34.470
there was this bimodal migration,
things have accelerated

00:58:34.470 --> 00:58:36.440
in the last 20 years.

00:58:36.440 --> 00:58:38.840
And the way society was
shaped at that time,

00:58:38.840 --> 00:58:40.840
I talked about how all
of those people coming

00:58:40.840 --> 00:58:42.773
from the countryside came to the city,

00:58:44.010 --> 00:58:46.410
what happened at that time
was what happens oftentimes

00:58:46.410 --> 00:58:48.810
when you have very different
people, all those energies

00:58:48.810 --> 00:58:51.640
gathering in a city, it's paradoxical,

00:58:51.640 --> 00:58:54.860
but it's almost as if this concentration

00:58:54.860 --> 00:58:59.300
of all the different parts
of Martinique in cities

00:58:59.300 --> 00:59:04.123
that happened maybe after the (indistinct)

00:59:04.123 --> 00:59:07.810
and then after World War II,
where also kind of the source

00:59:07.810 --> 00:59:12.350
of all those ideas and because
people were living together

00:59:12.350 --> 00:59:13.590
and they were agreeing with each other,

00:59:13.590 --> 00:59:15.660
they were all agreeing next to each other.

00:59:15.660 --> 00:59:20.030
And by this development that we have now,

00:59:20.030 --> 00:59:22.940
all those, there's like social economics,

00:59:22.940 --> 00:59:24.801
some of the socioeconomics progress

00:59:24.801 --> 00:59:27.070
have been made, maybe the
ability to travel more,

00:59:27.070 --> 00:59:31.660
maybe, and the fact that
people are living further away

00:59:31.660 --> 00:59:34.510
from each other, so this
fabric of society has changed.

00:59:34.510 --> 00:59:37.360
And at the same time, all
the tools that were created

00:59:37.360 --> 00:59:42.360
to foster this living
together have collapsed,

00:59:44.550 --> 00:59:46.350
and there's nothing to replace them.

00:59:49.010 --> 00:59:51.740
So when I came back,

00:59:51.740 --> 00:59:55.240
so I've been reading a lot because I come

00:59:55.240 --> 00:59:57.990
from people who were in the the fight,

00:59:57.990 --> 00:59:59.880
so you have to read very early on,

00:59:59.880 --> 01:00:01.910
you have to be sensitive
to some of the issues,

01:00:01.910 --> 01:00:03.510
you have to be knowledgeable about them,

01:00:03.510 --> 01:00:05.150
you need to be more and
more and more, more.

01:00:05.150 --> 01:00:10.150
That's almost like the
trend, but I realized

01:00:10.350 --> 01:00:13.670
when I was like 18 years old
that most of the knowledge

01:00:13.670 --> 01:00:15.670
that I was reading were in libraries

01:00:15.670 --> 01:00:19.200
where so you have to go to
this place or this place,

01:00:19.200 --> 01:00:21.920
but they were not readily available.

01:00:21.920 --> 01:00:23.720
There was nothing on
Wikipedia, for example,

01:00:23.720 --> 01:00:24.780
to put it simply.

01:00:24.780 --> 01:00:27.700
There was no program that was
made to make all those things

01:00:27.700 --> 01:00:31.510
that was knowledge that
was already constituted

01:00:31.510 --> 01:00:33.140
by previous generation,

01:00:33.140 --> 01:00:37.100
there was no effort at the
time to make it accessible.

01:00:37.100 --> 01:00:41.490
And this problem stayed with
us in the last 20 years or so.

01:00:41.490 --> 01:00:43.450
And maybe that was maybe an occasion

01:00:43.450 --> 01:00:47.850
because you don't have maybe
to have a cultural center

01:00:47.850 --> 01:00:50.540
because times change, but
maybe you could have thought

01:00:50.540 --> 01:00:51.767
about something that could be done

01:00:51.767 --> 01:00:53.791
for example on the
internet and everything.

01:00:53.791 --> 01:00:54.780
And what happened the last few years

01:00:54.780 --> 01:00:59.780
is just people trying to invest this space

01:01:01.720 --> 01:01:05.600
and trying to, and they
just grapple the ideas

01:01:05.600 --> 01:01:08.150
as the come, and this
produce maybe something

01:01:08.150 --> 01:01:13.150
that we do not understand
or find to be illegitimate.

01:01:15.540 --> 01:01:17.120
- Thank you Zaka.

01:01:17.120 --> 01:01:19.570
I want to bring into the conversation

01:01:19.570 --> 01:01:23.997
a question that we got
through the Q&amp;A function

01:01:25.850 --> 01:01:29.210
that is coming from Elodie Eugenie,

01:01:29.210 --> 01:01:32.840
and she says, thank you
for this rich discussion,

01:01:32.840 --> 01:01:36.130
you showed that this
is a complex situation

01:01:36.130 --> 01:01:40.890
and that we lack an overall
point-of-view perspective

01:01:40.890 --> 01:01:44.120
and sometimes knowledge of one battles

01:01:44.120 --> 01:01:48.700
with the ideas that (speaking
in foreign language)

01:01:48.700 --> 01:01:50.230
I think she's referring back

01:01:50.230 --> 01:01:54.640
to some of the discourse of
the (indistinct) movement.

01:01:54.640 --> 01:01:58.420
So the question she asked
is how could you people deal

01:01:58.420 --> 01:02:02.080
with some ego maybe, but moreover the fact

01:02:02.080 --> 01:02:05.880
that bringing context and
nuances comes with the label

01:02:05.880 --> 01:02:10.840
of (muffled speaking)
by Stockholm syndrome?

01:02:10.840 --> 01:02:15.840
And I think what she is
referring to is the tensions

01:02:17.220 --> 01:02:21.770
that exist at the level
of discourse and dialogue

01:02:21.770 --> 01:02:26.203
in terms of having a
productive conversation,

01:02:28.230 --> 01:02:32.823
within that context of
the post-summer of 2020.

01:02:40.999 --> 01:02:45.150
- I think it's actually,
it's a key question.

01:02:45.150 --> 01:02:49.180
It's a key issue, one that
we've tried to address

01:02:49.180 --> 01:02:52.010
and that will continue
in the coming years.

01:02:52.010 --> 01:02:54.500
First, I wanna specify something,

01:02:54.500 --> 01:02:57.780
actually a significant portion

01:02:57.780 --> 01:03:02.780
of that Martiniquan population
was actually really upset.

01:03:02.980 --> 01:03:06.100
Really upset in the sense
that, oh, this was disorder.

01:03:06.100 --> 01:03:07.750
Some people like order.

01:03:07.750 --> 01:03:12.750
Also the non-core reactions
to (laughing) the event

01:03:13.690 --> 01:03:15.940
and the fact that some
people were just like,

01:03:16.790 --> 01:03:19.110
including people who are strong supporters

01:03:19.110 --> 01:03:20.882
of the mayor of Fort-de-France

01:03:20.882 --> 01:03:22.453
were of the impression that the mayor

01:03:22.453 --> 01:03:24.310
was just being humiliated,
et cetera, were upset,

01:03:24.310 --> 01:03:27.700
some of them from lower
classes, some of them

01:03:27.700 --> 01:03:31.050
like from the very working
background and just thinking,

01:03:31.050 --> 01:03:32.630
who are these young people just coming

01:03:32.630 --> 01:03:35.690
insult us in our city in front of us?

01:03:35.690 --> 01:03:40.350
And that's part of the, so
actually array of reactions

01:03:40.350 --> 01:03:43.049
to the issue is actually really wide,

01:03:43.049 --> 01:03:46.040
and we might not be (laughing) attributed

01:03:46.040 --> 01:03:47.680
to a certain extent.

01:03:47.680 --> 01:03:49.100
The second thing is that it's difficult.

01:03:49.100 --> 01:03:50.820
It's difficult for a number of reasons

01:03:50.820 --> 01:03:55.340
because what we talk about

01:03:55.340 --> 01:04:00.340
is can often be, people can talk

01:04:00.600 --> 01:04:01.760
about the lack of legitimacy,

01:04:01.760 --> 01:04:06.420
that we might have delegitimized saying

01:04:06.420 --> 01:04:08.940
your own intellect, the one
that was coming back often

01:04:08.940 --> 01:04:13.500
was, oh, you intellectuals,
you there, you live far away

01:04:14.359 --> 01:04:15.740
from here, you're not from
here, you cannot talk,

01:04:15.740 --> 01:04:17.450
but all you intellectuals

01:04:17.450 --> 01:04:22.450
as if we are being a scholar
was kind of disconnecting you

01:04:23.810 --> 01:04:27.240
from knowing exactly the
society in the Martinique

01:04:27.240 --> 01:04:31.730
from coming from a very popular
and blue-collar backgrounds

01:04:31.730 --> 01:04:33.270
in the case of Martinique.

01:04:33.270 --> 01:04:35.290
it was not, is it (indistinct) I think

01:04:35.290 --> 01:04:38.145
that it's just, it's a
very progressive work.

01:04:38.145 --> 01:04:42.450
There is nothing that we can do

01:04:42.450 --> 01:04:46.020
to totally change this, and
it means kind of ignoring part

01:04:46.020 --> 01:04:48.370
of what's going on on social media,

01:04:48.370 --> 01:04:50.100
kind of ignoring part of this

01:04:50.100 --> 01:04:54.950
and just do like some
deep work, just advancing

01:04:54.950 --> 01:04:57.993
and doing stuff, organizing
things in the public.

01:04:58.850 --> 01:05:01.170
It's been difficult because
of the sanitary contact

01:05:01.170 --> 01:05:03.670
and we've been on the lockdown
in Martinique as well.

01:05:03.670 --> 01:05:08.200
Actually Zaka and a group of
other people, including myself,

01:05:08.200 --> 01:05:12.133
we created an organization
and Zaka is actually

01:05:12.133 --> 01:05:14.500
a driving force behind the whole project

01:05:14.500 --> 01:05:18.880
to just have public arenas
of discussion of all

01:05:18.880 --> 01:05:21.210
of this stuff, some
very intellectual stuff

01:05:21.210 --> 01:05:23.737
like the carnival, chlordecone,
all kinds of things

01:05:23.737 --> 01:05:26.540
and to do it all over the
territory and invite people

01:05:26.540 --> 01:05:31.087
to just to talk and exchange
and do, and we'll do that.

01:05:31.087 --> 01:05:33.460
We have got from our history teachers

01:05:33.460 --> 01:05:36.460
in high schools and secondary school

01:05:36.460 --> 01:05:41.040
who launched (indistinct)
which is an online platform,

01:05:41.040 --> 01:05:43.820
like doing small formats, video formats

01:05:43.820 --> 01:05:45.120
on the local history.

01:05:45.120 --> 01:05:48.000
And I think it's a
multiplication of initiatives

01:05:48.000 --> 01:05:52.040
on parts of people who
have been kind of powered

01:05:52.040 --> 01:05:54.430
by what was going on that
is going to do something.

01:05:54.430 --> 01:05:57.530
And I think that we have
to recreate the spaces

01:05:57.530 --> 01:05:58.450
that have disappeared.

01:05:58.450 --> 01:05:59.520
We have to recreate them.

01:05:59.520 --> 01:06:02.540
And at least that's the
drive that we have (laughing)

01:06:02.540 --> 01:06:05.373
right now to do that
kind of things, mm-hmm.

01:06:06.670 --> 01:06:08.120
- Yeah, and I would add to this

01:06:08.120 --> 01:06:13.120
that having a critical
outlook on the issues

01:06:13.280 --> 01:06:17.680
and taking an analytical view is essential

01:06:17.680 --> 01:06:22.680
to the movement actually being understood.

01:06:24.680 --> 01:06:29.680
And it is not attempts...

01:06:29.980 --> 01:06:34.980
Attempts at explicating the
movement and explicating

01:06:35.370 --> 01:06:38.910
its actions are not
attempts at undermining it,

01:06:38.910 --> 01:06:41.820
but rather at taking
stock of the significance

01:06:41.820 --> 01:06:43.450
of those actions.

01:06:43.450 --> 01:06:47.430
And as scholars, whether or not we agree

01:06:47.430 --> 01:06:49.080
with any particular action

01:06:49.080 --> 01:06:54.080
is really not the point
necessarily, but it is as scholar

01:06:54.640 --> 01:06:57.180
and as citizens and as people
from Martinique, I think

01:06:57.180 --> 01:07:02.180
it is essential that we really
think about in a rigorous way

01:07:04.150 --> 01:07:06.640
the stakes and the issues
and the consequences

01:07:06.640 --> 01:07:10.830
of his actions as a community also

01:07:10.830 --> 01:07:13.570
because it is shaping
us for the way forward,

01:07:13.570 --> 01:07:16.270
and we want to do it in a thoughtful way,

01:07:16.270 --> 01:07:20.430
and we want to do it in
a way that makes us agent

01:07:20.430 --> 01:07:25.430
of that set of events, rather
than just people reacting

01:07:26.280 --> 01:07:30.680
to outside forces perhaps,
or people reacting to events.

01:07:30.680 --> 01:07:35.280
And for me, it is really
what is at the heart

01:07:35.280 --> 01:07:38.080
of the intellectual challenge.

01:07:38.080 --> 01:07:41.960
And taking a step back is
not Stockholm syndrome,

01:07:41.960 --> 01:07:44.220
it's not talking the language

01:07:44.220 --> 01:07:49.220
of some oppressor (muffled
speaking) is bringing people

01:07:50.810 --> 01:07:53.990
in front of their
responsibilities with regard

01:07:53.990 --> 01:07:56.900
to the political significance
of what they're doing,

01:07:56.900 --> 01:07:58.900
with regard to the historical significance

01:07:58.900 --> 01:08:03.006
what they're doing, and also appreciate

01:08:03.006 --> 01:08:06.983
and underline and shed light
on what is happening also.

01:08:12.513 --> 01:08:14.720
- I was raised with a very simple,

01:08:14.720 --> 01:08:17.940
and I think this sentence you
find it in pretty much all

01:08:17.940 --> 01:08:20.993
of the emancipation
movements, knowledge is power.

01:08:23.408 --> 01:08:27.280
If you wanna know what
you want to achieve,

01:08:27.280 --> 01:08:28.790
if you want to actually achieve something,

01:08:28.790 --> 01:08:30.930
you need to know what you want to achieve.

01:08:30.930 --> 01:08:32.530
You need to understand how to get there,

01:08:32.530 --> 01:08:34.610
you need to understand
the means to get there,

01:08:34.610 --> 01:08:37.250
you need to know what
works and what doesn't,

01:08:37.250 --> 01:08:39.850
and you need to know what was achieved

01:08:39.850 --> 01:08:43.800
before you and what you
need to do after that.

01:08:43.800 --> 01:08:45.340
That's very simple.

01:08:45.340 --> 01:08:49.700
It's not Stockholm syndrome,
is just knowing where you want

01:08:49.700 --> 01:08:52.050
to go and trying to find
out what you wanna go.

01:08:52.935 --> 01:08:54.040
And for that, you need to build your maps,

01:08:54.040 --> 01:08:55.958
you need to have your tools,

01:08:55.958 --> 01:09:00.958
and those tools are not
always physical tools.

01:09:00.960 --> 01:09:04.370
That's very simple, I might say (laughing)

01:09:04.370 --> 01:09:05.203
- Thank you.

01:09:05.203 --> 01:09:08.430
We have another question
(muffled speaking)

01:09:08.430 --> 01:09:10.210
who is also thanking us

01:09:10.210 --> 01:09:13.450
for this rich discussion, and asking,

01:09:13.450 --> 01:09:15.730
I wonder if the panelists could talk

01:09:15.730 --> 01:09:17.970
about the relationship of the struggle

01:09:17.970 --> 01:09:21.530
over historical memory and
social justice activism

01:09:21.530 --> 01:09:24.470
in Martinique with similar discourses

01:09:24.470 --> 01:09:26.750
or activism in Guadeloupe.

01:09:26.750 --> 01:09:30.170
Having visited both places,
she says, I was struck

01:09:30.170 --> 01:09:33.330
by the similarities and
differences and the role

01:09:33.330 --> 01:09:35.720
that the new Museum of History and Culture

01:09:35.720 --> 01:09:39.482
in Pointe-a-Pitre plays
in the conversation point.

01:09:39.482 --> 01:09:41.682
Pointe-a-Pitre is the
capital of Guadeloupe.

01:09:44.700 --> 01:09:46.146
- Maybe just, if your words,

01:09:46.146 --> 01:09:49.910
there's a very interesting book,

01:09:52.690 --> 01:09:55.327
social movements and the role
of trade unions in Martinique

01:09:55.327 --> 01:09:57.020
and Guadeloupe and the common point

01:09:57.020 --> 01:10:00.280
but also the differences
and the level of integration

01:10:00.280 --> 01:10:04.627
of trade unions, cultural
movements in Martinique

01:10:05.520 --> 01:10:07.600
while the work of representing workers

01:10:07.600 --> 01:10:11.800
whilst still is speaking
being taken on by trade unions

01:10:11.800 --> 01:10:15.000
in Martinique as well but it's
been also taken on largely

01:10:15.000 --> 01:10:17.580
by political parties
and especially the BPM.

01:10:17.580 --> 01:10:19.220
And in Guadeloupe, you have like this sort

01:10:19.220 --> 01:10:21.830
of like super integrated movement

01:10:24.720 --> 01:10:27.340
that is a movement of different of workers

01:10:27.340 --> 01:10:30.510
but it's also a movement
of a cultural movement.

01:10:30.510 --> 01:10:32.730
When they go to court to defend workers,

01:10:32.730 --> 01:10:34.643
they speak Creole because
in Martinique you speak,

01:10:34.643 --> 01:10:36.260
In Guadeloupe you speak Creole.

01:10:36.260 --> 01:10:40.410
You have like a whole very
integrated work that's been done,

01:10:40.410 --> 01:10:42.780
which means that the sites of social

01:10:42.780 --> 01:10:45.190
or political socialization
are not exactly the same.

01:10:45.190 --> 01:10:49.610
It was not cultural,
cultural, or in the open.

01:10:49.610 --> 01:10:52.000
It was also within organizations

01:10:52.000 --> 01:10:55.300
that are still pretty
powerful in Guadeloupe.

01:10:55.300 --> 01:10:57.670
That's something that we saw in 2009.

01:10:57.670 --> 01:11:00.390
The book I'm referring to is a book

01:11:00.390 --> 01:11:05.390
by (indistinct) movement, O-D-I-N,

01:11:07.870 --> 01:11:09.810
and the (indistinct) movement
in Martinique as well.

01:11:09.810 --> 01:11:13.210
But you really see that
the political education

01:11:13.210 --> 01:11:16.280
of a number of people in
Martinique on memory issues

01:11:16.280 --> 01:11:19.160
at the same level on
workers' rights is actually

01:11:19.160 --> 01:11:21.740
within organizations
that are very integrated,

01:11:21.740 --> 01:11:23.540
which is not exactly the structure

01:11:23.540 --> 01:11:24.940
that we have in Martinique.

01:11:24.940 --> 01:11:26.730
That's part of the answer

01:11:26.730 --> 01:11:31.730
because I'm not a specialist
of the Guadeloupe.

01:11:32.190 --> 01:11:36.050
What I, also I've been very interested,

01:11:36.050 --> 01:11:39.460
in fact I was invited once in a festival

01:11:39.460 --> 01:11:41.530
in Guadeloupe for the commemoration

01:11:41.530 --> 01:11:43.487
of the insurrection of
slaves in Guadeloupe

01:11:43.487 --> 01:11:45.400
and the abolition of
slavery in Guadeloupe,

01:11:45.400 --> 01:11:49.130
and actually the three or
four days of commemorations

01:11:49.130 --> 01:11:52.650
where also the occasion
to talk about the lack

01:11:52.650 --> 01:11:55.910
of hospital in Guadeloupe,
about workers' rights, about...

01:11:55.910 --> 01:11:58.520
So there was like this
sort of very memory moment,

01:11:58.520 --> 01:12:01.750
but then there was Casey,
who is a hip hop singer

01:12:01.750 --> 01:12:04.300
of Martiniquan origin who
was invited to Guadeloupe

01:12:04.300 --> 01:12:07.950
to, think that she was so
come back to Guadeloupe

01:12:07.950 --> 01:12:11.280
and come and have this show
in front of the audience

01:12:11.280 --> 01:12:12.690
that is also your audience.

01:12:12.690 --> 01:12:15.860
You had workers from the hospital,
you had, so it was a sort

01:12:15.860 --> 01:12:19.840
of very integrated also moment
during which people discussed

01:12:19.840 --> 01:12:24.330
about memory and about
some very contemporary

01:12:24.330 --> 01:12:25.650
and hot topics.

01:12:25.650 --> 01:12:29.220
And I just feel that this
sort of spaces did not exist

01:12:29.220 --> 01:12:34.220
as such now in Martinique,
but I might be wrong.

01:12:34.730 --> 01:12:36.630
I don't know what Zaka think about it.

01:12:39.830 --> 01:12:43.018
- No, you're absolutely
right, absolutely right.

01:12:43.018 --> 01:12:47.880
It's very, what's interesting

01:12:47.880 --> 01:12:50.500
about comparing different
communities in the diaspora

01:12:50.500 --> 01:12:54.820
in general is to see
how it heads and flows.

01:12:54.820 --> 01:12:58.023
Sometimes you have a
community who's very much,

01:13:00.340 --> 01:13:03.110
like avid guard in its structures,

01:13:03.110 --> 01:13:05.410
discourses, how they localize

01:13:05.410 --> 01:13:08.430
or how they think about
things, and then it just kind

01:13:08.430 --> 01:13:10.420
of crumbles and then it
transmits somewhere else,

01:13:10.420 --> 01:13:12.940
and then maybe sometimes
it comes back after after.

01:13:12.940 --> 01:13:15.693
And that's very interesting
to to understand.

01:13:16.930 --> 01:13:19.887
As for the history, I think
the paradox between Martinique

01:13:19.887 --> 01:13:24.200
and Guadeloupe is that they both have

01:13:24.200 --> 01:13:27.960
this very strong trade union tradition.

01:13:27.960 --> 01:13:32.520
And in Guadeloupe, the
emancipation fight stayed mostly

01:13:32.520 --> 01:13:34.513
within those trade unions,

01:13:35.750 --> 01:13:40.750
and then into a more military
or more violent movements

01:13:40.800 --> 01:13:45.000
that have today disappeared
mostly, disappeared actually,

01:13:45.000 --> 01:13:47.140
while in Martinique the transforming

01:13:47.140 --> 01:13:51.617
to localized political
parties with different visions

01:13:53.070 --> 01:13:57.200
in terms of policy and
roles, you have those layers

01:13:57.200 --> 01:14:00.730
like PPF, for example, what's
kind of independentist,

01:14:00.730 --> 01:14:02.610
that then became an autonomist party.

01:14:02.610 --> 01:14:06.000
Then the generation of the
'70s became independentist,

01:14:06.000 --> 01:14:07.720
then within the independentist movements,

01:14:07.720 --> 01:14:09.653
there was different schools.

01:14:10.810 --> 01:14:14.100
the Marxist and the Trotskyist
and the all are the parties

01:14:14.100 --> 01:14:17.410
but actually they were
not from the independence.

01:14:17.410 --> 01:14:20.750
So you have this very
complicated, then of course most

01:14:20.750 --> 01:14:23.610
of those parties that was
kind of pro- emancipation

01:14:23.610 --> 01:14:25.183
were coming from the left wing.

01:14:26.160 --> 01:14:29.680
And the scope in Martinique
is basically promise

01:14:29.680 --> 01:14:34.633
assimilation party toward
total independence.

01:14:35.490 --> 01:14:37.680
But today in 2020, and that's something

01:14:37.680 --> 01:14:41.810
that's very interesting when
you think about what happened

01:14:41.810 --> 01:14:46.810
this year, for, I think
since the early '90s,

01:14:46.860 --> 01:14:47.953
there was two political parties

01:14:47.953 --> 01:14:49.793
have been powered Martinique mostly.

01:14:50.750 --> 01:14:54.600
There is almost no
pro-assimilation forces.

01:14:54.600 --> 01:14:58.460
Even right-wing parties in
Martinique are for some form

01:14:58.460 --> 01:15:03.460
of autonomy or now are even
like say they're Martiniquan.

01:15:08.850 --> 01:15:10.500
And that's what it is.

01:15:10.500 --> 01:15:13.430
The two parties that were
in power has been mostly

01:15:13.430 --> 01:15:15.740
PPM, so the party for Aime Cesaire,

01:15:15.740 --> 01:15:17.490
the Progressive Party of Martinique,

01:15:17.490 --> 01:15:21.570
which is autonomist, but
autonomist toward the goal

01:15:21.570 --> 01:15:24.620
of being dependent in some form.

01:15:24.620 --> 01:15:26.690
And the independentist parties.

01:15:26.690 --> 01:15:30.350
Right now in Martinique, the
head of the regional council

01:15:30.350 --> 01:15:34.910
of, sorry, of the Autonomist
Council of Martinique

01:15:34.910 --> 01:15:37.840
is one of the founders

01:15:37.840 --> 01:15:40.980
of the Independentist
Movement of Martinique.

01:15:40.980 --> 01:15:42.640
So you have this paradox.

01:15:42.640 --> 01:15:46.320
Right now, the political
spectrum, its majority

01:15:46.320 --> 01:15:51.290
is pro towards some form of
autonomy or independence,

01:15:51.290 --> 01:15:52.880
which means people are mostly conscious

01:15:52.880 --> 01:15:54.500
about the current situation,

01:15:54.500 --> 01:15:58.490
about ideological debates
and about our relationship

01:15:58.490 --> 01:16:00.490
with France and our history with France.

01:16:01.850 --> 01:16:05.160
Creole was illegal almost
talking Creole in the '80s,

01:16:05.160 --> 01:16:07.550
nowadays there's programs in the schools

01:16:07.550 --> 01:16:09.860
and there's radio programs and everything.

01:16:09.860 --> 01:16:11.750
Things have changed.

01:16:11.750 --> 01:16:16.370
But at the same time,
there is this deep feeling

01:16:16.370 --> 01:16:21.370
of something that is not
present, and this is the space

01:16:21.920 --> 01:16:24.653
that I live in and that we work in.

01:16:28.850 --> 01:16:29.683
- Thank you.

01:16:30.900 --> 01:16:35.900
We have another question
from Ariela Gross.

01:16:38.521 --> 01:16:40.970
That is, she says, I'm wondering

01:16:40.970 --> 01:16:43.210
if you could speak to the interplay

01:16:43.210 --> 01:16:44.880
between the memory politics

01:16:44.880 --> 01:16:48.090
happening in Martinique
(muffled speaking) Hexagon.

01:16:48.090 --> 01:16:51.823
What she means by that I
think is our European friends,

01:16:52.980 --> 01:16:55.350
both at the level of the French government

01:16:55.350 --> 01:16:59.240
as well as Black Life Matter
on the streets in Paris.

01:16:59.240 --> 01:17:02.573
How much interaction is
there between the movements?

01:17:09.090 --> 01:17:11.810
- Well, thank you so much
Ariela for your question.

01:17:11.810 --> 01:17:14.180
It's a complex one actually (laughing)

01:17:14.180 --> 01:17:19.000
But well, what's interesting
is that you clearly have

01:17:19.000 --> 01:17:22.280
local dynamics of memory
regarding the memory of slavery

01:17:22.280 --> 01:17:26.290
in Martinique and the more
national dynamics we have.

01:17:26.290 --> 01:17:28.520
If you look at the commemoration

01:17:28.520 --> 01:17:31.420
of the Centennial of the
abolition of slavery,

01:17:31.420 --> 01:17:35.580
you clearly had a strong
reluctance on part of France,

01:17:35.580 --> 01:17:37.440
people pushing, including
communist, people pushing

01:17:37.440 --> 01:17:39.650
for the commemoration in 1948.

01:17:39.650 --> 01:17:41.850
We have local commemorations

01:17:41.850 --> 01:17:46.760
of, and days that are
off doing which people,

01:17:46.760 --> 01:17:50.380
I don't know (speaking
in foreign language)

01:17:50.380 --> 01:17:52.640
Holiday, it's supportive or whatever.

01:17:52.640 --> 01:17:55.010
But it's, you have those days

01:17:55.010 --> 01:18:00.010
in Martinique, May 22nd, and
Guadeloupe, May 27, et cetera.

01:18:00.310 --> 01:18:04.860
So there are some local
dynamics regarding all of this,

01:18:04.860 --> 01:18:06.370
and you have the national dynamics

01:18:06.370 --> 01:18:10.180
that have in mainland, Hexagon or France

01:18:10.180 --> 01:18:11.810
that have accelerated

01:18:11.810 --> 01:18:16.793
since the end of the 1990s,
plus the (indistinct)

01:18:19.920 --> 01:18:21.890
at the beginning of the years 2000.

01:18:21.890 --> 01:18:26.310
And so you have this sort
of catching up on part

01:18:26.310 --> 01:18:30.750
of the national arena, public arena,

01:18:30.750 --> 01:18:35.750
you had like the
(indistinct) in 2006 or 2007,

01:18:36.950 --> 01:18:39.360
the National Day for Commemoration,

01:18:39.360 --> 01:18:41.200
that really people don't care

01:18:41.200 --> 01:18:42.710
about when they're in
Martinique and in Guadeloupe,

01:18:42.710 --> 01:18:46.820
May 10th is a date that
is getting more important

01:18:46.820 --> 01:18:48.830
in mainland France, that's important,

01:18:48.830 --> 01:18:50.860
but that is not clearly
the focus of people

01:18:50.860 --> 01:18:52.890
in Martinique and Guadeloupe, they know

01:18:52.890 --> 01:18:55.300
that it's not the day
that's really acknowledged,

01:18:55.300 --> 01:18:57.600
the day that's very
acknowledged, that's important

01:18:57.600 --> 01:19:01.000
is May 22nd for Martinique, et cetera.

01:19:01.000 --> 01:19:03.840
But then there is an articulation

01:19:03.840 --> 01:19:05.687
on a number of level.

01:19:05.687 --> 01:19:08.112
There's an articulation because
ever since there have been

01:19:08.112 --> 01:19:12.029
(speaking in foreign language)

01:19:13.180 --> 01:19:15.513
People, including local
historians from Martinique

01:19:15.513 --> 01:19:19.190
have been part of the commissions,

01:19:19.190 --> 01:19:20.850
have been part of the discussion.

01:19:20.850 --> 01:19:23.210
So there is some, maybe not enough,

01:19:23.210 --> 01:19:25.330
but there's a form of incorporation

01:19:25.330 --> 01:19:30.330
of people in the
Martinique and Guadeloupe,

01:19:30.820 --> 01:19:34.573
the Reunion Island, French
Guiana and the national level.

01:19:35.650 --> 01:19:39.170
Now, in terms of the interaction

01:19:39.170 --> 01:19:40.760
between the Black Lives Matter,

01:19:40.760 --> 01:19:43.830
the anti-racist movement in Paris

01:19:43.830 --> 01:19:47.520
and how there was sort of like the big,

01:19:47.520 --> 01:19:51.730
sort of a Black Lives Matter
movement in France as well,

01:19:51.730 --> 01:19:53.260
it was actually at the exact same time

01:19:53.260 --> 01:19:54.093
that there was all

01:19:54.093 --> 01:19:59.093
of these movements regarding
the claudicant and toppling

01:19:59.330 --> 01:20:00.430
of the statues in Martinique.

01:20:00.430 --> 01:20:03.810
So even though people looked
at them and connected them

01:20:03.810 --> 01:20:06.250
into some of the ideas
and there was something

01:20:06.250 --> 01:20:07.840
and it also brought legitimacy

01:20:07.840 --> 01:20:11.060
to what was going on in
Martinique, it seems to me

01:20:11.060 --> 01:20:14.460
that there was a disconnect
between the youth,

01:20:14.460 --> 01:20:17.900
including the black youth
of French Caribbean descent

01:20:17.900 --> 01:20:20.000
that was in the streets of Paris,

01:20:20.000 --> 01:20:25.000
and what was going on in
Martinique and Guadeloupe.

01:20:25.997 --> 01:20:29.190
And in Paris, it seems
to me that the connection

01:20:29.190 --> 01:20:31.460
with the memory of slavery was not direct.

01:20:31.460 --> 01:20:33.780
That was really the thing
of like police violence

01:20:33.780 --> 01:20:38.310
and racism, addressing racism
in a very straightforward way,

01:20:38.310 --> 01:20:40.740
and that even though the memory

01:20:40.740 --> 01:20:42.657
of slavery is part of the
discussion and the conversation,

01:20:42.657 --> 01:20:44.990
it was not directly the thing.

01:20:44.990 --> 01:20:46.770
But I think that we would need to talk

01:20:46.770 --> 01:20:50.750
about it very extensively (laughing)

01:20:53.170 --> 01:20:54.380
- Thank you.

01:20:54.380 --> 01:20:56.320
I don't know Zaka, if you have a.

01:20:56.320 --> 01:20:58.907
- I don't really have anything to add.

01:20:58.907 --> 01:21:00.900
- Oh, okay (laughing)

01:21:00.900 --> 01:21:04.280
Let me turn to another
question that I think

01:21:04.280 --> 01:21:08.920
is a name, Kaiama Glover.

01:21:08.920 --> 01:21:12.030
Excuse me if I mispronounced the name.

01:21:12.030 --> 01:21:13.720
Given the remarkable fact

01:21:13.720 --> 01:21:15.530
that this conversation is happening

01:21:15.530 --> 01:21:19.700
in English at Yale among
three Francophone thinkers,

01:21:19.700 --> 01:21:23.370
I guess our accents
give us away (laughing)

01:21:23.370 --> 01:21:27.550
I wonder whether you all
might (muffled speaking)

01:21:27.550 --> 01:21:30.060
on the intermediary or mediating space

01:21:30.060 --> 01:21:33.390
of the United States
when it comes to academic

01:21:33.390 --> 01:21:38.323
and/or activist work in the
Hexagon and in Martinique.

01:21:39.890 --> 01:21:44.210
And I can start just
with a word as the person

01:21:44.210 --> 01:21:49.210
organizing this forum indeed
in English and indeed at Yale.

01:21:49.950 --> 01:21:54.840
And my thinking was that
the set of events happening

01:21:54.840 --> 01:21:59.840
in Martinique and the
debate going on around it

01:21:59.950 --> 01:22:02.510
that has been quite robust
in the last few months,

01:22:02.510 --> 01:22:05.650
and we've talked about it a
lot, we've heard about it a lot,

01:22:05.650 --> 01:22:10.120
was of such a relevance
way beyond the confines

01:22:10.120 --> 01:22:14.610
of Martinique, that the broader
worlds has much to learn

01:22:14.610 --> 01:22:18.630
from the ways in which
this particular section

01:22:18.630 --> 01:22:21.830
of the diaspora is functioning right now

01:22:21.830 --> 01:22:26.830
within this particular climate
that is declining itself

01:22:27.500 --> 01:22:30.917
globally and also
hemispherically in the Americas.

01:22:31.780 --> 01:22:35.470
And so I thought it was a very relevant

01:22:35.470 --> 01:22:40.270
and maybe in a bit of a Martiniquan hubris

01:22:40.270 --> 01:22:43.170
that everybody should know about
what's happening (laughing)

01:22:43.170 --> 01:22:48.170
in Martinique because it
gives much food for thought.

01:22:48.180 --> 01:22:53.180
So I don't mean by it a
gesture of appropriation

01:22:55.980 --> 01:22:59.200
of the debate from Martinique,
but on the opposite

01:22:59.200 --> 01:23:01.320
to shed light, to bring a spotlight

01:23:01.320 --> 01:23:02.920
on something very interesting

01:23:02.920 --> 01:23:07.197
and I think important going on there.

01:23:07.197 --> 01:23:11.310
There is also the level of
the fact that I am based

01:23:11.310 --> 01:23:16.070
in the U.S., and it is
true that the diaspora

01:23:16.070 --> 01:23:21.070
from Martinique is extending
away beyond European friends

01:23:21.260 --> 01:23:25.760
to many corners of the
world, and this is a debate

01:23:25.760 --> 01:23:30.520
that is reaching these corners
because of it, but the fact

01:23:30.520 --> 01:23:35.350
about displacement is
also part of the situation

01:23:35.350 --> 01:23:40.350
that is coming to such a
boiling point in this moment.

01:23:42.900 --> 01:23:47.900
- The way I see Kaiama's
question and comments

01:23:48.610 --> 01:23:53.610
is actually we have to go
through (laughing) U.S. spaces

01:23:54.480 --> 01:23:55.630
to actually discuss,

01:23:55.630 --> 01:23:59.270
this is the very formal and setting.

01:24:00.950 --> 01:24:03.660
It's like it's a certain talk I'm giving

01:24:03.660 --> 01:24:05.787
in U.S. universities by people

01:24:05.787 --> 01:24:10.210
who are interested and
sometimes, and in France,

01:24:10.210 --> 01:24:13.810
apart from the (speaking
in foreign language)

01:24:13.810 --> 01:24:16.610
most people except, and that's again

01:24:16.610 --> 01:24:19.970
something very interesting in
Anglo-American departments,

01:24:19.970 --> 01:24:22.880
and actually my faculty position

01:24:22.880 --> 01:24:25.210
is in an Anglo-American departments.

01:24:25.210 --> 01:24:27.610
Apart from that, it's
true that the interest

01:24:27.610 --> 01:24:30.860
is (sighs) people are kind of interested

01:24:30.860 --> 01:24:35.860
but the fact that we
have to, in that case,

01:24:36.500 --> 01:24:39.810
there was clearly this,
everything that you said, Cecile,

01:24:39.810 --> 01:24:42.540
is totally true.

01:24:42.540 --> 01:24:44.890
And at the same time, the lack of spaces

01:24:44.890 --> 01:24:48.760
in the French case to
address this question

01:24:48.760 --> 01:24:52.020
is just so great.

01:24:52.020 --> 01:24:54.200
I don't even know the objective
in English that I can use

01:24:54.200 --> 01:24:56.143
to say that.

01:24:57.970 --> 01:25:00.600
It was really interesting
because last year,

01:25:00.600 --> 01:25:05.430
I attended an event
organized by Kaiama Glover

01:25:05.430 --> 01:25:07.413
at Barnard on the Digital Caribbean,

01:25:08.640 --> 01:25:10.860
and I was like, I wanted to cry

01:25:10.860 --> 01:25:15.500
because then you had this room
full of Caribbean scholars

01:25:15.500 --> 01:25:18.030
or scholars of the Caribbean gathering,

01:25:18.030 --> 01:25:21.910
explaining how the
digital had been important

01:25:21.910 --> 01:25:24.100
as a space to address a number

01:25:24.100 --> 01:25:27.220
of issues that didn't have
enough space in academia.

01:25:27.220 --> 01:25:30.730
And I was like, so you don't
realize how much space I have

01:25:30.730 --> 01:25:33.570
compared to what we
have in mainland France.

01:25:33.570 --> 01:25:36.420
And it was a really,
it was a difficult one

01:25:36.420 --> 01:25:39.070
because even though I
feel really Martinique,

01:25:39.070 --> 01:25:40.670
and I've been here for 22 years,

01:25:42.360 --> 01:25:46.610
and this kind of space that
we're struggling to create,

01:25:46.610 --> 01:25:51.610
are sometimes given to us
much in a much easier fashion

01:25:52.170 --> 01:25:55.280
through U.S. universities,
and that's a reality,

01:25:55.280 --> 01:25:57.130
and it's not going to get better

01:25:57.130 --> 01:26:02.130
given the current context in
France regarding this kind

01:26:02.320 --> 01:26:06.170
of studies that even that
had just started to rise

01:26:06.170 --> 01:26:10.130
and that you already have
forces that want to crack them.

01:26:10.130 --> 01:26:12.300
So what, thanks you so much
for (laughing) the question

01:26:12.300 --> 01:26:15.193
and Cecile for the space to
actually address all of this.

01:26:16.910 --> 01:26:19.833
- Yeah, it's an incredible paradox.

01:26:21.031 --> 01:26:23.520
And I know very personally,

01:26:23.520 --> 01:26:28.330
it's been, so I was living in
Taiwan for 10 years almost,

01:26:28.330 --> 01:26:29.550
and I came back in Martinique,

01:26:29.550 --> 01:26:31.930
so of course I went for my
study then I went to work

01:26:31.930 --> 01:26:34.590
and then I went very far
away, and I came back

01:26:34.590 --> 01:26:39.590
four years ago and right away
my idea was to create a space

01:26:40.470 --> 01:26:44.580
and bring together writers
and creators and fiction,

01:26:44.580 --> 01:26:48.110
nonfiction, and have an,
so I created a review,

01:26:48.110 --> 01:26:50.110
and I of course decided to make it online

01:26:50.110 --> 01:26:53.000
because it would be easier
to reach out to the diaspora,

01:26:53.000 --> 01:26:55.443
welcome to the 21st century, et cetera.

01:26:56.920 --> 01:26:58.950
This was so difficult.

01:26:58.950 --> 01:27:03.300
It's like, of course there
was people were willing

01:27:03.300 --> 01:27:04.334
to give me a space and
say, hey, there's this guy

01:27:04.334 --> 01:27:08.980
who's trying to do a cultural,
an ID review in Martinique,

01:27:08.980 --> 01:27:11.490
and push the envelope,
reach all the writers

01:27:11.490 --> 01:27:15.460
and, but there was an
overwhelming majority of people

01:27:15.460 --> 01:27:16.650
four years ago who explained to me

01:27:16.650 --> 01:27:18.860
that it made no sense,
there was no need for it.

01:27:18.860 --> 01:27:21.173
And that was in the local site.

01:27:22.800 --> 01:27:24.290
But I pushed on through,

01:27:24.290 --> 01:27:26.570
and then it turned out like some

01:27:26.570 --> 01:27:30.700
of the things that were coming
out from the review came

01:27:30.700 --> 01:27:33.800
even before some of the events
that have been happening

01:27:33.800 --> 01:27:37.550
politically, whether here or
in France actually sometimes.

01:27:37.550 --> 01:27:41.940
And for example, the analysis

01:27:41.940 --> 01:27:45.083
about the different statutes,
I already did in 2017.

01:27:46.888 --> 01:27:51.888
And so somehow the review
and the project, the idea

01:27:54.380 --> 01:27:56.440
that we need spaces to talk about, we need

01:27:56.440 --> 01:27:58.730
to produce knowledge, we
need to produce fiction,

01:27:58.730 --> 01:28:02.370
we need to integrate all
those different narratives,

01:28:02.370 --> 01:28:04.620
and we need to talk about how
we relate to the Caribbean,

01:28:04.620 --> 01:28:07.100
how all of the things
are connected, Africa,

01:28:07.100 --> 01:28:10.920
North America, France, and
we need to produce ourselves.

01:28:10.920 --> 01:28:15.353
Those spaces only makes sense kind of now.

01:28:16.830 --> 01:28:21.830
But locally, I received
no help, no funding,

01:28:23.590 --> 01:28:24.423
no public funding.

01:28:24.423 --> 01:28:27.120
I went and see all the
institutions, I told them,

01:28:27.120 --> 01:28:29.820
we should do events, we should
do this, we should do that.

01:28:29.820 --> 01:28:32.143
I basically had to fund them myself.

01:28:33.320 --> 01:28:38.100
I couldn't find any help
from local authorities,

01:28:38.100 --> 01:28:42.280
local institutions that have resources,

01:28:42.280 --> 01:28:47.280
that are headed by Martiniquans,
and they did not see

01:28:48.860 --> 01:28:51.830
the need for those spaces of debates.

01:28:51.830 --> 01:28:54.460
And this year in the last few months

01:28:54.460 --> 01:28:57.740
that suddenly there's this
urgency to realize it.

01:28:57.740 --> 01:29:02.740
So that begs the question of
once you achieve some form

01:29:03.320 --> 01:29:05.933
of emancipation, some form of autonomy,

01:29:06.780 --> 01:29:08.900
it doesn't end there,
and you need to think

01:29:08.900 --> 01:29:11.170
about what is structural?

01:29:11.170 --> 01:29:14.810
What is what is actually
very important for societies

01:29:14.810 --> 01:29:16.920
to progress and advance
and keep on pushing

01:29:16.920 --> 01:29:19.310
and keep on existing and getting stronger

01:29:19.310 --> 01:29:20.710
and getting more vibrant?

01:29:20.710 --> 01:29:22.210
And that also involves

01:29:23.330 --> 01:29:26.150
having your own institutions of cultures.

01:29:26.150 --> 01:29:29.490
And that's what they call soft power.

01:29:29.490 --> 01:29:32.130
It's a very real thing in diplomacy

01:29:32.130 --> 01:29:34.810
and political relationships
between countries.

01:29:34.810 --> 01:29:37.110
The United States have mastered them,

01:29:37.110 --> 01:29:40.740
and we are sadly smaller
players and maybe one day

01:29:40.740 --> 01:29:42.743
we will realize how important it is.

01:29:45.680 --> 01:29:47.600
- Well, thank you so much.

01:29:47.600 --> 01:29:52.600
And incredibly we are out
of time for this webinar.

01:29:54.430 --> 01:29:56.980
I want to thank our audience

01:29:56.980 --> 01:30:01.970
for the many excellent thought-provoking

01:30:01.970 --> 01:30:04.900
and courageous questions that came

01:30:04.900 --> 01:30:07.610
through the question and answers tool.

01:30:07.610 --> 01:30:11.820
We will make sure to send
them to the panelists

01:30:13.050 --> 01:30:17.600
after the event and so
that they are made aware

01:30:17.600 --> 01:30:21.810
of what you guys shared with us.

01:30:21.810 --> 01:30:26.653
But let me just thank you
for coming and listening on.

01:30:27.640 --> 01:30:31.630
Thank you to Audrey and Zaka for playing

01:30:31.630 --> 01:30:36.200
with us at Yale and Rhythm
for this conversation.

01:30:36.200 --> 01:30:40.167
And thank you to Matthew Tanico and Rhythm

01:30:41.220 --> 01:30:46.003
and Prof. Pitti for kind
of lending us this forum.

01:30:47.340 --> 01:30:52.300
I am also happy

01:30:52.300 --> 01:30:56.230
to say that the session has
been recorded and we hope

01:30:56.230 --> 01:31:01.080
to be able to share it on the
Rhythm YouTube channel soon.

01:31:01.080 --> 01:31:06.080
So we will make sure to
let who has registered

01:31:06.480 --> 01:31:11.040
about where to go and find us to relive

01:31:11.040 --> 01:31:15.360
this (laughing) moment
together and share it.

01:31:15.360 --> 01:31:16.880
Thank you very much.

01:31:16.880 --> 01:31:17.713
- Thank you.

01:31:17.713 --> 01:31:18.546
- Thank you so much.

01:31:18.546 --> 01:31:19.379
I'm looking for the questions,

01:31:19.379 --> 01:31:21.560
I took a quick look at
the various question,

01:31:21.560 --> 01:31:24.010
it was created lots of food for thought.

01:31:24.010 --> 01:31:26.820
Thank you so much Cecile
and Zaka (laughing)

01:31:26.820 --> 01:31:28.224
- Thank you.

01:31:28.224 --> 01:31:29.807
- [Zaka] Thank you.

